[The doctor, Dr. Smith, enters the examination room where Teresa Sullivan is sitting, looking concerned.]

Dr. Smith: Hi, Teresa. Thank you for coming in today. I understand you've been experiencing some right elbow pain lately.

Teresa: Yes, doctor. It started about four days ago, and it's been getting worse. I've been trying to ignore it, but it's really affecting my daily activities.

Dr. Smith: I see. Can you tell me more about what's been happening? What's been causing you to experience this pain?

Teresa: Well, I've been using a pottery wheel a lot lately to make a bowl for my son's birthday. I think it might be related to that. I've been feeling some discomfort and stiffness in my elbow, especially when I'm using the pottery wheel.

Dr. Smith: I see. Have you noticed any other symptoms, such as numbness or tingling in your arm or hand?

Teresa: No, just the pain and stiffness. It's been waking me up at night if I roll over, and it's been affecting my ability to work on the pottery wheel.

Dr. Smith: Okay. Have you tried any over-the-counter pain relievers or ice packs to help with the pain?

Teresa: No, I haven't. I was hoping to find a more natural way to deal with it.

Dr. Smith: I understand. Based on your symptoms and the x-ray results, I'm going to diagnose you with medial epicondylitis, which is a common condition caused by overuse and damage to the tendons in your arm. I'm going to recommend that you wear a sling on your right arm while you're awake to provide support and reduce the strain on your elbow. I'll also have you take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for the next week to help reduce the inflammation.

Teresa: Okay, that sounds good. What about the pottery wheel? Can I still use it?

Dr. Smith: I'm afraid not, at least not for the next two weeks. We need to give your elbow some time to heal. I'll also have you ice your elbow for 20 minutes three times a day to help reduce the inflammation.

Teresa: Okay, I can do that. What about my diabetes and blood pressure? Are those okay?

Dr. Smith: Your blood sugar levels seem to be under control, and your blood pressure is well-managed. However, I do want to encourage you to take your medication consistently and make sure to check your blood sugar levels regularly. I'll also order a hemoglobin A1c test to get a more accurate assessment of your long-term blood sugar levels.

Teresa: Okay, I'll make sure to do that.

Dr. Smith: One more thing. I want to refer you to diabetic counseling to help you manage your diabetes more effectively. We'll also schedule a follow-up appointment in two weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin A1c test and discuss your progress.

Teresa: Okay, that sounds good. Thank you, doctor.

Dr. Smith: You're welcome, Teresa. Take care of yourself, and I'll see you in two weeks.<|eot_id|>
