**Doctor:** Hi, Gregory. I'm Dr. Smith. I'm glad you came to see me today about your right ankle pain and swelling. Can you tell me more about what happened?

**Patient:** Yeah, doc. I slipped and fell on the ice yesterday while taking out the trash. I couldn't move at first, and a friend had to help me up. Since then, I've been icing and elevating my ankle, and taking ibuprofen for the pain. It's helped a bit, but I'm still limping.

**Doctor:** I see. Did you notice any numbness or tingling in your foot after the fall?

**Patient:** No, nothing like that. Just a lot of pain and swelling.

**Doctor:** Okay. Have you had any previous injuries to your right ankle?

**Patient:** No, nothing like this. I'm pretty active, though. I play soccer in the community league.

**Doctor:** Ah, great. So, I've taken a look at your ankle, and I'm going to confirm that you've got a right ankle sprain. We did take some x-rays, and they didn't show any signs of a fracture. I'm going to prescribe some treatment to help you recover.

**Patient:** Okay, what's the plan?

**Doctor:** First, I want you to keep your ankle elevated, especially when you're sitting. We'll also get you an Aircast to help stabilize it. You'll need to use crutches for the next day or two, and then we'll gradually transition to weight-bearing as tolerated. For pain control, you can continue taking NSAIDs as needed. And don't forget to keep icing your ankle.

**Patient:** Got it. And when can I expect to feel better?

**Doctor:** You should start seeing significant improvement in the next two weeks. We'll reassess your progress at your follow-up appointment in two weeks, and we'll determine your return-to-play date for soccer.

**Patient:** Sounds good. And what if my symptoms get worse or change before then?

