dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, beverly. how are you?
[patient] i'm doing well, thank you.
[doctor] great. i'm glad to see you. so, i know we're here for your annual exam, but i know you have a history of type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure. so, have you been doing okay with that?
[patient] yes, i have. i've been doing pretty well.
[doctor] okay, good. and you've recently started working at a bakery this summer, correct?
[patient] yes, that's right. i started working at a bakery in june.
[doctor] okay. and how's that been going?
[patient] it's been going well. i really like it. i've been trying to improve my diet since i've been working there.
[doctor] okay. and how's your diabetes been since you started working there?
[patient] it's been a little bit up and down.
[doctor] okay. and what do you think is causing that?
[patient] i think it's because of the diet. i've been eating a lot more sweets since i started working at the bakery.
[doctor] okay. and are you still taking your metformin twice a day?
[patient] yes, i am. i haven't missed any doses.
[doctor] okay. and how about your blood pressure? how's that been?
[patient] my blood pressure has been good.
[doctor] okay. and you're still taking the lisinopril, correct?
[patient] yes, i am.
[doctor] okay. and how many milligrams?
[patient] 10 milligrams once a day.
[doctor] okay. and how have you been sleeping?
[patient] i've been sleeping well. i get a full 8 hours a night.
[doctor] okay, that's good. and have you had any thoughts of self-harm or suicidal ideations?
[patient] no, i haven't.
[doctor] okay, good. so, i reviewed your results, and your hemoglobin a1c is a little bit elevated. and we'll talk about that in a minute. but first, i just want to go through some of your vital signs and physical exam. so, your blood pressure is 130 over 78. your heart rate is 72. your respiratory rate is 16. and your temperature is 98.6. all of those are good. let's go ahead and do a quick physical exam. so, your heart is regular rate and rhythm. i do appreciate a 2 out of 6 systolic ejection fraction murmur. your lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi. your abdomen is soft, non-tender, and non-distended. and your neurological exam is normal. your grip strength is equal bilaterally. your neurologically intact. so, let's talk a little bit about your diabetes. so, your blood glucose levels have been a little bit uncontrolled. and i know you're still taking the metformin twice a day. but, you do admit to a little bit of dietary indiscretion with an increase in your sweets intake. and your most recent hemoglobin a1c was elevated. so, what i would like to do is i want you to check your fasting blood glucose levels each morning, and then just call those in to the office in about two weeks. and then, we'll follow up in about two months to discuss further treatment recommendations. does that sound okay?
[patient] yes, that sounds fine.
[doctor] okay. and then, for your blood pressure, it seems to be stable. so, we'll just continue you on the lisinopril 10 milligrams once a day. and i want you to continue watching your diet, okay?
[patient] okay, i will.
[doctor] okay, great. well, i'm glad to see you. do you have any questions for me?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay, great. well, i'll see you in a couple months.
[patient] okay, thank you.
[doctor] you're welcome. take care. }}<|eot_id|>
