dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hey, how are you?
[patient] hey, i'm okay. how about you?
[doctor] i'm doing well, thank you. i see that you're here for some back pain. what happened?
[patient] yeah, i was at a birthday party and i ran my back into the railing of the staircase and twisted my back.
[doctor] oh, that sounds like it could be quite painful. are you able to sit up straight or do you have to lean back?
[patient] i can sit up straight, but leaning back is a bit uncomfortable.
[doctor] okay. and are you experiencing any pain radiating down your legs?
[patient] no, not at all.
[doctor] okay. does it hurt when you lie flat?
[patient] yes, that's when it hurts the most.
[doctor] and what about twisting motions?
[patient] yes, those make it worse.
[doctor] okay. have you tried anything for the pain?
[patient] i've been taking ibuprofen.
[doctor] okay. have you ever had any back surgery?
[patient] yes, i had a lumbar fusion.
[doctor] okay. how are you doing since the fusion?
[patient] i'm doing okay. it's been about two years now.
[doctor] okay, good. have you experienced any pain since the fusion?
[patient] not really, just some stiffness.
[doctor] okay. well, let me just take a look at you. hey, dragon, show me the exam. [doctor] okay, so on examination, your back has good range of motion without pain. your straight leg raise is negative. sensation is intact. so, based on my exam, it looks like you have a lumbar spine strain. your x-rays show no obvious signs of an acute fracture, and your hardware from your fusion is in good position. so, for your back pain, i would recommend some ice and heat. you can alternate those. i'm also going to prescribe you motrin 800 milligrams three times a day with food. and if you continue to have problems, you can follow up with me.
[patient] okay, sounds good. thank you.
[doctor] you're welcome. take care.
[patient] you too. }}<|eot_id|>
