[patient] it's been going on for the last month, probably.
[doctor] okay. and what happens when it starts hurting?
[patient] it starts hurting after a couple of laps.
[doctor] okay. and have you injured the knee or had any trauma to it?
[patient] no, i don't think so. i mean, i'm not really sure.
[doctor] okay. and how about your back pain? how's that been?
[patient] it's been doing really well. it's been intermittent, but it's been going well.
[doctor] okay. and any pain radiating down your legs?
[patient] no, no.
[doctor] okay. and how about your depression? how are you doing with that?
[patient] i'm doing really well with it. the medication and therapy have been really helpful. i've been trying to get outside more and it's been really helping.
[doctor] okay. and how's your sleep and eating?
[patient] i'm sleeping and eating well.
[doctor] okay. and any concerns about hurting yourself or others?
[patient] no, none at all.
[doctor] okay. and how about your carpal tunnel? how's that been?
[patient] it's doing really well. i've been getting full range of motion and it's felt a lot better.
[doctor] okay. all right. well, i'm just gon na do a quick physical exam. i'm gon na start with your neck. your neck looks good. no thyromegaly or cervical lymphadenopathy. your lungs sound clear. your heart is regular rate and rhythm. i don't hear any murmurs, gallops, or rubs. there are no extra heart sounds. your lower extremities show no edema. but i do appreciate some pain with patellar inhibition and pain with range of motion of the right knee. i'm gon na take a look at your knee x-ray. hey, dragon, show me the right knee x-ray. okay. so the x-ray of your right knee is unremarkable. there is no arthritis or any other abnormality. so let's talk a little bit about my assessment and plan for you. so for your joint pain, i think you have chondromalacia of the right patella. you've been more active, but you're not aware of any injury to the knee. your recent x-rays were normal, but you did have some discomfort on exam. so for that, i'm gon na prescribe you ibuprofen 600 milligrams four times a day with food. i do want you to do some straight leg raising exercises that will strengthen your quadriceps. i would encourage you to participate in physical activity that's less straining on your joints, such as swimming freestyle laps, or to stop activity all together. if your pain doesn't improve in a couple of weeks, we'll follow up.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] for your chronic back pain, it looks like that's stable and well-controlled at this time. you should continue swimming regularly once your knee is feeling better.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and for your depression, you're doing well with your current regimen. you should continue with that. and for your carpal tunnel syndrome, it looks like you're doing well with that as well. i don't think i need to make any changes at this time.
[patient] okay. sounds good.
[doctor] any questions for me?
[patient] no, i think that's it.
[doctor] all right. well, i'll see you in a couple of weeks to check on your knee.
[patient] okay. thank you, doctor.
[doctor] you're welcome. }}<|eot_id|>
