dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, brittany, how are you?
[patient] i'm doing okay. how about you?
[doctor] good to see you. i know it's been a while. i know we had you last year with the irritable bowel syndrome, and i know you've been having some issues with some of your symptoms.
[patient] yeah, it's been up and down.
[doctor] okay. so, tell me what's been going on.
[patient] so, i have the flare ups of the abdominal pain. i've been having some nausea and vomiting.
[doctor] okay. and have you tried to take the bentyl that we had prescribed for you?
[patient] yeah, i have. i've been taking it, but it doesn't really help much.
[doctor] okay. and i know you had a prescription for reglan that you got from the er. are you taking that?
[patient] i was. i was taking it as needed for the nausea, but i've stopped taking it.
[doctor] okay. all right. and have you noticed if there's any foods that you eat that seem to make your symptoms worse?
[patient] yeah, i think i ate some shrimp yesterday and it made me have both upper and lower abdominal pain and i was bloated. and then i had some chicken alfredo and diarrhea.
[doctor] okay. all right. and how often are you having the diarrhea?
[patient] probably like two to three times a day.
[doctor] okay. all right. and is that with every meal or is it after certain meals?
[patient] it's after certain meals. it's after chicken alfredo and after drinking.
[doctor] okay. and do you drink a lot of alcohol?
[patient] i do drink, but i don't think it's really contributing. i only drink two drinks when i'm out, either tequila or brown liquor, and i don't drink that much. i drink a martini every once in a while.
[doctor] okay. all right. and do you notice if you're stressed or if you're eating certain foods that make it worse?
[patient] yeah, i do notice that it's worse when i'm stressed.
[doctor] okay. all right. and how many times a week would you say you have the flare ups?
[patient] it's pretty much every day, but it's not as bad as it was last year.
[doctor] okay. all right. well, let's do a quick physical exam. your vital signs look good. your lungs are clear. your heart is regular. your abdomen is soft, non-tender. there's no masses or organomegaly, and your bowel sounds are normal. so, you're still passing two to three formed stools a day, right?
[patient] yeah, i'm passing two to three formed stools a day.
[doctor] okay. and do you feel like you're sleeping well?
[patient] not really. i don't sleep well.
[doctor] okay. all right. well, i think we need to start you on a low dose fiber supplement, like citrucel, daily, and some probiotics to help with the gas and bloating. and i know you've been taking the bentyl, and i want you to continue that up to four times a day. however, i want you to stop taking the reglan and we're going to start you on zofran for the nausea.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i know we talked about starting some neuromodulators like elavil and buspar last time. i think we should start you on elavil 25 milligrams at bedtime. it's an antidepressant that can help with your symptoms.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and then you can use the bentyl 20 milligrams 30 to 45 minutes before you eat at a restaurant. and if you're still having nausea, you can use the zofran as needed.
[patient] okay. okay.
[doctor] and i think we need to work on some dietary and lifestyle modifications. try to reduce the greasy fatty foods and alcohol consumption.
[patient] okay. i'll try.
[doctor] all right. so, i want you to send me a message in a month or so and let me know how you're doing. if you have any side effects of the elavil, if you need a dose adjustment, let me know.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i want to see you back in a month or sooner if you're not feeling better.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] all right. thanks, brittany.
[patient] thank you. }}<|eot_id|>
