dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, miss carol. how are you?
[patient] i'm doing okay. how are you?
[doctor] i'm doing well. it's good to see you. so, i know that we saw you in the hospital, um, for the heart attack that you had, um, about a month ago. how are you feeling?
[patient] i'm feeling better. i'm still a little short of breath.
[doctor] okay. and are you still having any chest pain?
[patient] no chest pain.
[doctor] okay. all right. and you're still taking your brilinta, your blood thinner, and your aspirin?
[patient] yes.
[doctor] okay. good. and, you know, i know that we had a lot of stress about that, you know, you were having the heart attack and the cath lab was backed up and, um, i know that you were worried that you were going to be in the hospital for a long time, but we were able to get you in and get you a stent and get you out of here pretty quickly, which was good. and i know that you had a lot of questions about that. do you have any questions about that?
[patient] no, i think you explained everything.
[doctor] okay. good. and, you know, i know that you had a lot of questions about your heart failure and how we're going to manage that. so, let's go ahead and talk about that. um, i know that you were in the hospital for a couple of days and we didn't do an echocardiogram, but, you know, we had one done about a week ago, and it looks like your heart is functioning at about 35% of its normal function, which is not uncommon after a heart attack, but it should improve over time. so, we'll just have to watch that. um, i know that you've been retaining some fluid. have you noticed any swelling in your legs?
[patient] yeah, my legs are swollen.
[doctor] okay. and that's because of the heart failure. um, so, i'm gon na go ahead and prescribe you some lasix, um, to help with that. i'm also gon na start you on a medication called aldactone, which is a water pill, and i wan na get some blood work done to see how you're doing. um, and i wan na repeat an echocardiogram in about two months to see how your heart's doing.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. and, you know, i know that we had talked about you wanting to start exercising, and i think that's a great idea. i'm gon na go ahead and send you to cardiac rehab for that, and i think that will be really helpful for you.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. and, you know, i know that you have a lot of questions about your diet, and i know that you like to eat meat, but you know, i think that we should try to cut back on that a little bit and watch your salt intake and things like that. um, i'm also gon na send you to a nutritionist to talk about that. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. and then, i know that you have your hypertension, and that's doing well. i'm gon na keep you on the lisinopril and, you know, i think that you're doing a good job with that. and then, i know that you have your diabetes, and that's doing well. i'm gon na keep you on the metformin and, you know, i think that you're doing a good job with that. and then, i know that we have to talk about your coronary artery disease. um, i know that you had a stent in 2018, and i think that we'll just continue to monitor that. you're doing a good job with your aspirin and your brilinta, and i think that that's going to help you.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. so, let's go ahead and get you set up with that cardiac rehab, and i'll get you an appointment with the nutritionist. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. great. well, it's good to see you.
[patient] same here.
[doctor] okay, take care.
[patient] you too. }}<|eot_id|>
