dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hey, john. how are you doing today?
[patient] hey, doctor. i'm doing alright. how about you?
[doctor] i'm doing well, thank you. so, i see you're here for a follow-up. how have you been feeling since your heart attack?
[patient] i've been doing pretty well. i'm still taking all my medications and trying to watch what i eat.
[doctor] that's good to hear. i know you had a heart attack about six months ago, and you have a history of coronary artery disease and diabetes. have you had any problems since then?
[patient] not really. i've been feeling alright.
[doctor] okay, that's good. have you had any chest pain or shortness of breath when you're exercising?
[patient] no, i haven't.
[doctor] okay. and you had a stent placed in your left anterior descending artery?
[patient] yes, that's right.
[doctor] okay. and you're still taking aspirin and brilinta?
[patient] yes, i am.
[doctor] and you're also taking metoprolol, fish oil, and crestor?
[patient] yes, that's correct.
[doctor] okay. and how have you been feeling in terms of your blood pressure?
[patient] my blood pressure's been pretty good.
[doctor] okay. your blood pressure today is 127 over 80, which is good. and you don't have any muscle aches?
[patient] no, i don't.
[doctor] okay. and how about dizziness or headaches?
[patient] i have been having some dizziness and headaches.
[doctor] okay. and any depression or anything like that?
[patient] i've been feeling a little down. i'm just having a hard time adjusting.
[doctor] okay. i see. and have you had any thoughts of hurting yourself or anybody else?
[patient] no, no.
[doctor] okay. all right. let's go ahead and do a quick physical exam. your vital signs look good. let's listen to your heart. i do hear a slight three out of six systolic ejection murmur at the left base, but that's something that's been present in the past. i don't think it's a problem. your lungs are clear. let me just check your legs. you do have a one plus non-pitting edema in your lower extremities, but that sounds like it might be from your medications. let's take a look at your results. your creatinine levels are stable, which is good. i see that your most recent hemoglobin a1c was 16. we need to work on getting that down to six. and i know you're taking insulin, but i'm gon na contact your primary care doctor to see if we can adjust that. i'm gon na order another hemoglobin a1c today. i'm gon na go ahead and order a repeat one and then we can discuss the results next time you come in. i'm gon na also refer you to a dietitian for some diabetes education. i think it's really important for your heart health. and i'm also gon na refer you to a psychiatrist for your depression. i know you're feeling a little down and it's understandable since your heart attack. but it's important to get that under control. i'm gon na order a refill of your metoprolol and crestor and you can continue taking your aspirin and brilinta for the next six months. and i'm gon na refer you to cardiac rehab. it's really beneficial in helping you become more active and confident with exercising. do you have any questions for me?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay. do you have any hobbies or interests that you enjoy?
[patient] i'm a big san francisco 49ers fan.
[doctor] okay. i'm a seahawks fan, so we'll have to agree to disagree on that. but i'm glad to hear you have a hobby. do you have a family or any friends?
[patient] i don't really have a support system. i live with my dog.
