dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, alexis. how are you?
[patient] i'm doing okay. how are you?
[doctor] i'm doing well, thank you. so, i know the nurse told you about dax. i'd like to tell dax a little bit about you. you're a 33-year-old female here for the evaluation of right hand pain. is that right?
[patient] yes, that's right.
[doctor] okay. so, you know, you've been experiencing some numbness and tingling in your right hand, correct?
[patient] yes, i have.
[doctor] okay. so, when did this start?
[patient] about six months ago.
[doctor] okay. and when you first started noticing this, was it constant, or did it come and go?
[patient] it started out as coming and going. now it's pretty much constant.
[doctor] okay. and it's just in the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd digits of your right hand?
[patient] yes, those three.
[doctor] okay. and it's been constant for the past month or so?
[patient] yeah, it has.
[doctor] okay. and what kind of activities, you know, do you perform at your job that you think might be causing this?
[patient] i work on a keyboard all day, so i'm constantly using a mouse and typing.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any trouble sleeping?
[patient] no, no trouble sleeping.
[doctor] okay. and what have you been doing for your symptoms?
[patient] i've been taking ibuprofen and just resting.
[doctor] okay. and does the ibuprofen and rest help?
[patient] yeah, that's the most help i've gotten.
[doctor] okay. okay. and have you noticed any other symptoms, like pain or swelling in your hand?
[patient] no, nothing like that.
[doctor] okay. and i know you said you've been taking ibuprofen. what dosage are you taking?
[patient] um, i'm not sure. i just take whatever the bottle says.
[doctor] okay. and how many times a day are you taking it?
[patient] just when it bothers me.
[doctor] okay. so, you know, just when it bothers you, okay. and what time of day is that?
[patient] usually when i'm on the computer.
[doctor] okay. and how long have you been taking it for?
[patient] just for the past month or so.
[doctor] okay. and how long does it help for?
[patient] until the next time it bothers me.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any other medical conditions or other symptoms that you've noticed?
[patient] no, nothing else.
[doctor] okay. and any other surgeries or medical procedures in the past?
[patient] no, nothing.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any other family members with similar symptoms?
[patient] no, not that i know of.
[doctor] okay. all right. well, let me just do a quick physical exam of your hand. i'm just gon na move your wrist around. does that hurt?
[patient] yes, it does.
[doctor] okay. okay. and if i press right here, does that hurt?
[patient] yes, that hurts too.
[doctor] okay. and if i press right here, does that hurt?
[patient] yes, that hurts as well.
[doctor] okay. and if i press here, does that hurt?
[patient] yes, that one does too.
[doctor] okay. and if i press here, does that hurt?
[patient] yes, that one does as well.
[doctor] okay. okay. and i'm just gon na check your grip strength. okay?
[patient] sure.
[doctor] okay. and your grip strength is a little bit weaker on the right hand compared to the left. okay? and i'm just gon na check your sensation. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. and i'm just gon na do a quick test called tinel's sign. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. and that's positive. okay. so, what does that mean?
[patient] i'm not sure.
[doctor] so, what that means is that there is some irritation of the nerve in your wrist, which is causing some of the numbness and tingling that you're experiencing.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and the symptoms that you're experiencing are pretty typical for something called carpal tunnel syndrome.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and that's when the median nerve gets compressed as it goes through the wrist, and it causes some of the symptoms that you're experiencing.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] so, we can treat this a few different ways. we can try some conservative treatment, like using a wrist splint at night, and i'd like to increase the dosage of the ibuprofen that you're taking to 600 milligrams four times a day.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i would like to order an emg/nerve conduction study, just to confirm what we suspect. and if that comes back positive, we might consider some physical therapy, or we might have to talk about surgery.
[patient] okay. and what kind of surgery?
[doctor] surgery would be a release of the carpal tunnel. it's a pretty straightforward surgery. it's about an hour, and you'll be able to use your hand again within a few days.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and you'll be back to work within a week or so.
[patient] okay. that sounds good.
[doctor] okay. so, do you have any questions for me?
[patient] no, i think you've covered everything.
[doctor] okay. great. well, i'm gon na go ahead and order that emg and the wrist splint. i'll have the nurse come in and get you set up for that. and then we'll follow up with the results.
[patient] okay. sounds good. thank you.
[doctor] you're welcome. }}<|eot_id|>
