dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, emma, how are you?
[patient] hi, doctor. i'm okay. how are you?
[doctor] i'm doing well. so, you know, we had talked about some abnormal labs that you had when you came in last time. and we're gon na go over those, but i know you had an insect bite, and we drew a lyme titer. and that came back positive.
[patient] yes, i did. i went hiking in the woods and i got bitten by an insect, and i thought it was a tick at first, but they said it was a spider.
[doctor] okay, so, and we're gon na talk about the lyme disease, but i know you're here today because you're not feeling well. so, what's going on?
[patient] i'm feeling really tired. i'm just lacking a lot of energy. and my joints are really swollen. i've been having some body aches. and i have this rash on my elbow.
[doctor] okay. and what does the rash look like?
[patient] it's like a bullseye. it's on my elbow and it's been there for about a week now.
[doctor] okay. and are you having any high fevers?
[patient] no, not really. just feeling really tired.
[doctor] okay. and how about your diet? how have you been eating?
[patient] not a lot. i've been eating crackers, just bland foods. i just don't feel like eating.
[doctor] okay. and how about any nausea or abdominal pain or diarrhea?
[patient] no, no abdominal pain or diarrhea, but i've been nauseous.
[doctor] okay. and regarding your diabetes, how have you been doing with that? have you noticed any changes in your blood sugars?
[patient] well, my sugars have been okay. they've been a little high or low sometimes, but nothing too out of range. i'm still taking my metformin.
[doctor] okay. and how about your blood pressure? have you been checking it at home?
[patient] yes, i have been. it's been good.
[doctor] okay. and how are you doing with the salty foods? have you been avoiding those?
[patient] yes, i have been. i'm still taking my lisinopril.
[doctor] okay. well, let's go ahead and do a physical exam. so, on your physical exam, your abdomen is a little warm to palpation, but it's not tender. and i don't see any evidence of cellulitis. your elbow joints are swollen and painful to palpation bilaterally. and i do see a small bullseye rash on your left abdominal quadrant.
[patient] yes, that's the one.
[doctor] okay. so, looking at your results, your wbc is within normal limits, and all other labs are normal, but your lyme titer is elevated, which is consistent with lyme disease.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] so, for your first problem, we're gon na go ahead and start you on doxycycline, 100 milligrams twice a day for three weeks. i'm gon na touch base with an infectious disease doctor to ensure that we don't need to do anything else. i want to see you again in one week for a follow-up.
[patient] okay, that sounds good.
[doctor] for your second problem, your hypertension, it's been well-controlled. you've been compliant with the dietary modifications, limiting your sodium intake. i'm going to go ahead and order a lipid panel. you will continue lisinopril 10 milligrams a day. i refilled that for you today.
[patient] okay, thank you.
[doctor] and for your third problem, your diabetes, you've noticed intermittent elevations in your blood glucose levels, but you've been compliant with the use of metformin. your diet has been limited due to the nausea from the lyme disease. i'm gon na go ahead and order a hemoglobin a1c to ensure that we don't need to make any medication changes.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] do you have any questions or concerns?
[patient] no, i think that sounds good.
[doctor] okay, great. i'm gon na go ahead and order those labs, and i'll see you in a week.
[patient] okay, thank you.
[doctor] you're welcome. }}<|eot_id|>
