dialogue is: {{ [doctor] so christopher watson is an 86 year old male who returns in follow up of adult hydrocephalus this is his first visit since undergoing shunt surgery on 8/1/2020. he is accompanied by his daughter theresa together they give his history.
[doctor] so christopher how are you doing?
[patient] i'm doing pretty good.
[doctor] good to hear.
[patient] i'm feeling pretty good.
[doctor] i'm glad to hear that. so i've been following you since may 2020 you presented with 8 to 10 years of progressive gait impairment cognitive impairment and decreased bladder control. we established a diagnosis of adult hydrocephalus with the spinal catheter protocol in june 2020 and you underwent shunt surgery on 8/1/2020. a medtronic strata programmable shunt in the ventriculoperitoneal configuration programmed at level 2.0 was placed. so i know theresa here is your daughter, i know you've been doing some physical therapy, how are you doing with that?
[patient] i'm doing okay.
[doctor] okay.
[patient] i'm doing pretty good.
[doctor] that's good. and so theresa how's he doing with the walking? is he able to walk more?
[patient] yeah, he is. he can walk to the dining room fine now, but he couldn't before.
[doctor] okay, good. and so he's been able to walk to the dining room now. and how about his balance? is that improved?
[patient] yeah, he's doing better with his balance.
[doctor] okay. and how about his bladder control? did he have some improvement there?
[patient] yeah, he did, but he's kind of leveled off now. he still wears a diaper.
[doctor] okay. and how about his thinking? has that improved at all?
[patient] yeah, i think so.
[doctor] okay. and so, and how would you say he's doing now? is he able to have conversations with you?
[patient] yeah, i can have a normal conversation with him again.
[doctor] okay, that's great. and so, have you had any headaches or pain at the shunt site or in the abdomen?
[patient] no, no headaches or pain.
[doctor] okay. and so, have you had any other illnesses or hospitalizations since i last saw you?
[patient] no, no.
[doctor] okay, good. so let's do a quick review of systems. so, any fever or unintentional weight loss?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and any urinary issues? any genital sores, decreased or painful output?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and any headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and any depression, excessive worrying or mood swings?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. all right. well, let's do a quick physical exam. so his vital signs are blood pressure 124 over 80, heart rate 64, respiratory rate 18, and his weight is 174 pounds and he rates his pain as zero out of 10. okay, so his head and face exam is intact. the shunt site is clean, dry, and intact and confirmed at a setting of 2.0. and on neurologic exam, his mental status, he's a pleasant and cooperative man who is able to converse easily though his daughter adds some details. i tested for recent and remote memory, attention span, concentration, and fund of knowledge. he scored 26 out of 30 on the mini mental status exam when tested with spelling and 25 out of 30 when tested with calculations. of note, he was able to get two of the three memory words with cuing and the third one with multiple choice. this was a slight improvement over his initial score of 23 out of 30 with calculations and 24 out of 30 with spelling and at that time he was unable to remember any memory words with cuing and only one with multiple choice. and on gait exam, i tested him using the tinetti assessment tool. he was tested without an assistive device and received a gait score of 6 to 8 out of 12 and a balance score of 12 out of 16 for a total score of 18 to 20 out of 28. this has slightly improved from his initial score of 15 to 17 out of 28. and on cranial nerves, his pupils are equal. extraocular movements are intact. face is symmetric. no dysarthria. motor is normal for bulk and strength. coordination is slow for finger to nose. and so, looking at his results, his ct scan of the head from 10/15/2020 shows a frontal horn span at the level of foramen of munro of 4.6 centimeters with a third ventricular contour that is flat with the span of 10 millimeters. by my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with just a tiny subdural collection. this was not noticed by the radiologist who stated no extra axial fluid collections. there is also substantial small vessel ischemic change. so his assessment is adult hydrocephalus, gait impairment, urinary incontinence and urgency, and cognitive impairment. so he has made some improvement with his hydrocephalus since shunt surgery. there is a tiny fluid collection in the right parietal region. i do not believe that this tiny amount of fluid is symptomatic, and it was not documented by the radiologist when he read the ct scan. so i know he asked me about whether he will be able to drive again. unfortunately, i think it is unlikely that his speed of movement will improve to a level that he will be able to pass a driver's safety evaluation, however, occasionally patients surprise me by improving enough over 9 to 12 months that they are able to pass such a test. i would certainly be happy to recommend such a test if i believe he is likely to pass it and he is always welcome to enroll in a driver's safety program without my recommendation, however, i think it is exceeding unlikely that he has the capability of passing this rigorous test at this time. i also think it is quite likely he will not regain sufficient speed of motion to pass such a test. so i had a long discussion with the patient and his daughter. we are all pleased that he has started to make some improvement with his hydrocephalus because of the fluid collection in the right parietal region, i would like to leave the setting at 2.0 for another three months before we consider changing the shunt. so i will see him back in three months. all right, thank you very much.
[doctor] thank you.
[patient] thank you.
[doctor] good to see you.
[patient] good to see you.
[doctor] all right, thank you.
[patient] thank you.
[doctor] thank you. }}<|eot_id|>
