dialogue is: {{ [doctor] dennis martin is a pleasant 46-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right heel pain. the onset of his pain began 10 days ago while training for a triathlon. he locates his pain to the posterior aspect of his heel, which radiates proximally into his calf. his pain is aggravated by working out and when he first gets up in the morning. if he sits for a long period, his pain will return upon standing. the patient states that he runs 10 kilometers in the morning. at lunchtime, he tries to get on a bike for about 45 minutes to an hour. in the evening, he goes to the aquatic center and swims for 30 minutes to 1 hour. the patient states that he runs 5 days a week. he adds that he alternates between a long run and a long bike ride on weekends. he currently takes one day off from his exercises. the patient states that he does not stretch at all. he denies any left leg pain.

[doctor] good morning, dennis. how are you doing today?
[patient] i'm doing alright. i'm here to get checked out for some heel pain that i've been having.
[doctor] okay. so, dennis, i see here that you've been having some pain in your right heel. can you tell me a little bit more about that?
[patient] sure. it started about 10 days ago. i've been training for a triathlon, and i was doing a lot of runs and bike rides, and i think it's just from overuse.
[doctor] okay. so, where exactly is the pain located?
[patient] it's in the back of my heel, and it kind of goes up into my calf.
[doctor] okay. is it painful when you're sitting for a long time?
[patient] yeah, if i sit for a long time and then get up, it hurts.
[doctor] okay. does it hurt when you're working out?
[patient] yeah, it does. it hurts when i'm working out, especially when i'm running.
[doctor] okay. and do you run every day?
[patient] yeah, i do. i run about 10 kilometers in the morning. i try to get on a bike at lunchtime for about 45 minutes to an hour. then, in the evening, i go to the aquatic center and swim for about 30 minutes to an hour. i run about 5 days a week. i take one day off, and then i'll do a long run and a long bike ride on the weekends.
[doctor] okay. do you stretch at all?
[patient] no, i don't.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any pain in your left leg?
[patient] no, just the right one.
[doctor] okay. let's go ahead and do a quick physical exam. i'm just going to check your right heel here. does that hurt?
[patient] yeah, that hurts.
[doctor] okay. let me press down on your heel. i'm going to push down on your heel here. how does that feel?
[patient] it hurts.
[doctor] okay. let me try to bend your ankle here. does that hurt?
[patient] a little bit.
[doctor] okay. and how about if i push your foot down? does that hurt?
[patient] yeah, it hurts.
[doctor] okay. i'm going to do a squeeze test here. i'm going to squeeze your calf. does that hurt?
[patient] yeah, it does.
[doctor] okay. let me check your left heel. how about if i push down on this one? does that hurt?
[patient] no, that doesn't hurt.
[doctor] okay. and let me bend your ankle here. does that hurt?
[patient] no, that doesn't hurt.
[doctor] okay. let me check your left calf. how about if i squeeze it?
[patient] no, that doesn't hurt.
[doctor] okay. so, dennis, i think what's going on here is that you've got some overuse of your right heel, which is causing the pain. i'm going to recommend that you take at least a week off from your training to recover from this. i'm also going to give you a referral for formal physical therapy to help strengthen your right lower extremities. i'm also going to give you some ibuprofen. i want you to take at least 3 tablets every 6 hours for pain. if you're not better after that, we can get an x-ray of your heel to see if there's anything structural that we need to look at.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] does that sound good?
[patient] yeah, that sounds good.
[doctor] okay. i'll have my assistant get the referral ready for you. i'll also give you some crutches if you need them.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] you're going to follow up with me in 7 to 10 days to check on your progress. does that sound good?
[patient] yeah, that sounds good.
[doctor] alright, dennis. thanks for coming in today. take care.
[patient] thanks, doctor. }}<|eot_id|>
