[doctor] okay. all right. well, let's go ahead and take a look at your foot. so, i'm just gon na take a look at your right foot here. i'm just gon na press on it a little bit. and i do see a one centimeter by two centimeter wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot, just proximal to the fifth mtp joint. i do see some yellow slough that's present with minimal granulation tissue. no surrounding erythema or cellulitis. there's no evidence of fluid collection. i don't see necrosis. no odor. i don't appreciate any bony exposure. no pain to palpation. your vital signs look good today. there is no fever. and your blood pressure is 140 over 70, your heart rate is 72, your respiratory rate is 18, and your temperature is 98.6. so, looking at your x-ray of your right foot, it does not show any evidence of osteomyelitis. so, let's talk a little bit about my assessment and my plan. so, i think that you have a diabetic foot ulcer. i would like to get an ankle brachial index on you to see if we can determine if there's good blood supply to your foot. i think it's important to do a debridement in the office today. i'd like to put you in a surgical shoe to provide increased support. i'd like to prescribe you collagenase ointment to apply to your wound once a day and cover it with a sterile dressing. and i think we should continue this until we see the ulcer decrease in size. and then you'll follow up with me in two weeks to check on your progress. does that sound okay?
[patient] okay. sounds good to me.
[doctor] okay. great. i'll go ahead and order the ankle brachial index. and then we'll get you set up for the debridement and get you into the shoe and get you started on the collagenase ointment. and then we'll see you back in two weeks. okay?
[patient] okay. thank you.
[doctor] you're welcome. }}<|eot_id|>
