dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, alexis. how are you doing today?
[patient] i'm doing okay. i'm here because i'm having some pain in my right hand.
[doctor] okay. and when did this start?
[patient] it started about six months ago, and it's been getting worse over the past month.
[doctor] okay. so it's been going on for about six months, but it's gotten worse over the last month. and what exactly is happening with your hand?
[patient] so, i'm experiencing some numbness and tingling in my first, second, and third fingers on my right hand.
[doctor] okay. and is that constant or does it come and go?
[patient] it's constant.
[doctor] okay. and when you say constant, does it happen all the time or is there any time of day when it's better or worse?
[patient] it's constant throughout the day, but it's worse when i'm working on my keyboard.
[doctor] okay. so you work on a keyboard, so that's your job, correct?
[patient] yes, i work on a keyboard all day.
[doctor] okay. and is it painful to use your keyboard?
[patient] it is painful. especially when i'm using the mouse.
[doctor] okay. and have you noticed any sleep disturbance because of this?
[patient] no, i haven't.
[doctor] okay. all right. so what have you been doing for the pain?
[patient] i've been taking ibuprofen and resting.
[doctor] okay. and has that been helping?
[patient] it's been helping a little bit.
[doctor] okay. and have you tried anything else?
[patient] no, that's about it.
[doctor] okay. let me do a quick physical exam on you. i'm gon na move your hand around a little bit and check some things. okay?
[patient] sure.
[doctor] all right. so i'm gon na press on the nerve here on the back of your wrist and see if that causes any numbness or tingling in your fingers. and when i press it, do you feel anything?
[patient] no, i don't feel anything.
[doctor] okay. all right. let's check your grip strength. i'm gon na have you squeeze my fingers here. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] all right. i'm gon na squeeze your fingers. okay, now i'm gon na release. okay, now squeeze. okay, now release. okay, so i'm gon na squeeze your fingers again. and now i'm gon na release. okay, now squeeze. and when i release, i'm gon na ask you to squeeze again. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] all right. so, you know, your grip strength is a little bit lower on your right hand compared to your left. does that feel different to you?
[patient] yes, it does.
[doctor] okay. all right. and let's check your wrist here. i'm gon na bend your wrist up and down and see if that causes any pain. okay?
[patient] yeah, that does hurt.
[doctor] okay. and when i bend it up, does it hurt?
[patient] yes, it does.
[doctor] okay. and when i bend it down, does it hurt?
[patient] yes, it does.
[doctor] okay. so, let's talk a little bit about what's going on. it sounds like you have something called carpal tunnel syndrome. it's a condition where the nerves in your wrist get compressed, and it causes numbness, tingling, and pain. it's very common in people who do a lot of typing or repetitive activities with their hands.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and it's really common in people who work on computers all day. so, for your symptoms, i'm gon na recommend a few things. first, i'm gon na prescribe you an ibuprofen, but i'm gon na increase the dose to 600 milligrams four times a day. i want you to take it with food, and i want you to take it every day.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] i'm also gon na recommend that you wear a wrist splint at night. that's going to help support your wrist and keep your wrist in a good position while you're sleeping. and i'm gon na order an emg/nerve conduction study for you. that's just a test to check the nerves in your arm. if that shows that there's a problem, we might need to talk about some other treatment options, like physical therapy or even surgery.
[patient] okay. sounds good.
[doctor] okay. and i want to see you back in about two weeks. does that work for you?
[patient] yeah, that works for me.
[doctor] okay. great. do you have any other questions for me?
[patient] no, i think you covered everything.
[doctor] okay. great. i'll see you in two weeks. take care. }}<|eot_id|>
