[patient] yeah, i'm still taking the medication. i'm still on the colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day as needed for flare-ups of my gout.
[doctor] okay. and how about your psoriasis? how's that doing?
[patient] it's under control.
[doctor] okay. and are you still using the steroid cream?
[patient] yeah, i'm still using the steroid cream.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any numbness or tingling in your hands?
[patient] sometimes i do. i just try to shake it out of my arms to relieve the numbness.
[doctor] okay. all right. well, let's do a quick physical exam. your vitals look good. your blood pressure is 120 over 80, your heart rate is 60, and your oxygen saturation is 98 percent on room air. your neck is supple without lymphadenopathy. your lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi. your heart is a regular rate and rhythm. your abdomen is soft. and i don't see any edema of your lower extremities. now, when i look at your right elbow, there is some edema and inflammation of the right olecranon. there is some tenderness to palpation of the olecranon bursa. you have some pain with pronation and supination. and you have pain with flexion and extension of your right arm. all right. so based on your exam, i think we can order an x-ray of your right elbow. and i'm going to prescribe you some meloxicam 15 mg once a day for the pain and swelling. and we will continue you on the colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day as needed for flare ups of your gout. and i'm going to continue you on the steroid cream for your psoriasis. any questions?
[patient] no, i don't have any questions.
[doctor] okay. great. well, do you have any other questions for me?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay. great. well, i'll see you next time.
[patient] okay, sounds good. }}<|eot_id|>
