dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, ms. thompson. how are you?
[patient] hi, doctor. i'm doing okay. i've been having some knee pain.
[doctor] okay. so, you know, you've been doing some swimming, and i know you've been trying to be more active, and i know you've had some knee pain. so, tell me a little bit more about that.
[patient] yeah. i started swimming a couple months ago. i've been doing a lot of swimming. i've been doing a lot of laps. i've been swimming, you know, five, six times a week.
[doctor] okay, that's great. and you've been doing like, how many laps?
[patient] um, i used to be able to do ten laps. now i can only do like two.
[doctor] okay. and then, um, what's going on with your knee when you're swimming?
[patient] so, after a couple of laps, it starts hurting.
[doctor] okay. and then, have you injured your knee at all, or is this just kind of like, you know, a pain that's been going on for a while?
[patient] i don't think i injured it. i just started doing more swimming.
[doctor] okay. and then, have you noticed any other pain anywhere else? like, in your back or anything?
[patient] um, my back has been doing really well. i've been having some intermittent back pain, but nothing's been really bothering me.
[doctor] okay, great. and then, you know, i know you've had some history of depression. how are you doing with that?
[patient] i'm doing really well. i've been sleeping well, eating well, and trying to get outside more, which is really helping me with my depression.
[doctor] okay, that's great. and then, um, you know, i know you had your carpal tunnel surgery a couple years ago. how's that doing?
[patient] that's doing really well. i'm getting full range of motion. it's felt a lot better.
[doctor] okay, great. um, so, let's go ahead and do a quick physical exam. so, um, your vitals look good. i don't see any fever, blood pressure's fine. um, on your physical exam, your heart is regular rate and rhythm. your lungs are clear. um, your neck is supple. you don't have any thyroid enlargement. um, your heart is regular. you don't have any murmurs, gallops, or rubs. um, your abdomen is soft. you don't have any tenderness. um, your lower extremities, there is no edema. um, you do have some pain with patellar inhibition. um, you do have some pain with range of motion of your right knee. um, your grip strength is equal bilaterally. um, your neurological exam is intact.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] so, i did order an x-ray of your right knee, and that looks unremarkable. um, so, you know, let's talk a little bit about your knee pain. um, you know, you said you've been swimming a lot, and you've been doing some laps, and then you said after a couple of laps, it starts hurting. um, have you tried to stop swimming, or does it just kind of get better as you continue?
[patient] it gets better as i continue.
[doctor] okay. and then, um, you know, i know you've had some history of chondromalacia of your patella. um, so, this could be related to that. um, have you taken any ibuprofen or any other medications for the pain?
[patient] i haven't taken anything yet.
[doctor] okay. so, um, let's talk a little bit about my assessment and plan. um, you know, for your first problem of your right knee pain, um, i think you have chondromalacia of your patella. um, i don't think you injured it, but i think you've been doing a lot of swimming and maybe it's just irritated. um, i want you to take some ibuprofen, six hundred milligrams, four times a day with food. um, i want you to do some straight leg raising exercises, um, which will help strengthen your quadriceps. um, i also want you to either stop swimming or try to do some other activities that are less straining on your joints, like freestyle swimming, or you can just stop. um, if your pain doesn't get better in a couple weeks, we'll follow up.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] um, for your second problem of your back pain, um, you know, i think that's stable and well-controlled. um, i want you to continue swimming once your knee feels better.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] um, and then for your third problem of your depression, um, i want you to continue with your current medications and therapy.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] um, and then for your last problem of your carpal tunnel syndrome, um, you know, i think that's stable status post surgery. um, i want you to continue with your current regimen. um, do you have any questions for me?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay. um, great. well, it was great to see you. um, i'll see you in a couple weeks. um, hey, dragon, finalize the note. }}<|eot_id|>
