dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, jordan. how are you?
[patient] hey, good. how are you?
[doctor] good. good. so, i see that you've been having some palpitations and high blood pressure. can you tell me more about what's been going on?
[patient] yeah. so, i went to the er last month for palpitations and high blood pressure. i think it was just a panic attack. i've been really stressed out at work.
[doctor] mm-hmm. okay. and did you have any other symptoms with that?
[patient] no, just palpitations and high blood pressure.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any episodes like that since then?
[patient] no, not since then. i've been keeping an eye on my blood pressure.
[doctor] okay. and what have you been doing to keep an eye on it?
[patient] i've been taking it at home every day. it's been running between 146 to 155.
[doctor] okay. and what have you been taking for your blood pressure?
[patient] i'm on amlodipine and benicar hct.
[doctor] okay. and you said you have a new primary care doctor. what did they start you on?
[patient] yeah. they started me on benicar hct.
[doctor] okay. and how long have you been on that?
[patient] about a month now.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any family history of high blood pressure?
[patient] yeah. several family members.
[doctor] okay. and what about salt intake? are you watching that?
[patient] yeah. i've been trying to cut back on it.
[doctor] okay. and how much salt are you taking in a day?
[patient] i don't know. i've been trying to cut back, but i'm not really sure.
[doctor] okay. well, let's try to get that down to less than 2000 milligrams a day. have you heard of the dash diet?
[patient] no, what's that?
[doctor] it's a diet that can help lower your blood pressure. i can give you some information on that. and i'm also going to add a medication to your regimen. it's called spironolactone. i want to start you on 100 milligrams once a day.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i want to monitor your potassium and sodium levels. i also want to check your blood pressure in two weeks.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i want you to try to walk on your treadmill as often as you can. at least 30 minutes most days.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and if we're not able to get your blood pressure down with the spironolactone, we may need to send you to a clinic that specializes in resistant hypertension. we'll also need to monitor your potassium levels and your sodium levels.
[patient] okay. what are the potential side effects of spironolactone?
[doctor] well, there's a risk of gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances. you also need to watch for dizziness. if you start to feel dizzy, that's a sign that you need to cut back on your salt intake.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i want you to go to the american heart association website. they have a lot of information on lowering your salt intake and increasing your potassium intake. and i want you to try to get your salt intake down to less than 2000 milligrams a day.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i want to see you back in two weeks to check your blood pressure and to check your basic metabolic panel.
[patient] okay. sounds good.
[doctor] and do you have any questions for me?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay. well, it was good seeing you today. i'll talk to you soon.
[patient] okay, thanks. }}<|eot_id|>
