dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, ms. ross, how are you doing today?
[patient] i'm doing okay. i'm a little nervous.
[doctor] i can understand that. so, you know, we've had a few new patients that have come in recently that have been experiencing some symptoms, and i think it's great that you're being proactive and coming in to get checked out. so, can you tell me a little bit about what's been going on?
[patient] yeah, so i've just been having some discomfort and a change in discharge, and there's an odor in my vaginal region, and the right side of my vagina is starting to become painful, but i don't have any fever or chills.
[doctor] okay. and when did you first start noticing these symptoms?
[patient] it was about a week ago, i think.
[doctor] okay. and have you noticed any changes in your discharge? like, is it thicker or thinner?
[patient] yeah, it's just changed, you know, the color, the smell, the consistency. i don't know.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any abnormal pap smears in the past?
[patient] yeah, i had one when i was like, 20, but all the ones since then, including the one like, six months ago, have all been normal.
[doctor] okay. and can you tell me a little bit about your sexual history? like, how many partners have you had?
[patient] um, i've had two, but my partner of three years has been having, uh, encounters with other people for quite some time, and i want to get checked out.
[doctor] okay. and how long have you been with your partner for three years?
[patient] three years.
[doctor] okay. and have you used any protection during those encounters?
[patient] no, we didn't.
[doctor] okay. and have you ever had any other sexual partners besides your current partner?
[patient] no, just him.
[doctor] okay. and when was the last time you had a pap smear?
[patient] um, six months ago.
[doctor] okay. and did you have any abnormal results?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and have you noticed any lesions or anything outside of your vagina?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and i'm going to go ahead and do a quick physical exam. i'm just going to check your vitals and do a pelvic exam. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] so, your blood pressure is 120 over 80, your heart rate is 60, and your temperature is 98.6. and on your pelvic exam, everything looks normal. i don't see any external lesions, and your labia majora and labia minora are normal. your physiological discharge is normal, and your cervix is normal, and your vaginal wall is normal, and i don't see any cervical motion tenderness or masses. and i did obtain some swabs for sexual transmitted disease testing.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] so, we're going to go ahead and get those results back, and we'll call you with the results. and we'll also order some tests to check for gonorrhea, chlamydia, trichomoniasis, syphilis, hiv, and hepatitis c.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and based on your history, you are at high risk for these diseases, and we want to make sure we cover all our bases. and i'll go ahead and explain the results to you when they come in. and if you have any questions or concerns, feel free to ask.
[patient] okay, thank you.
[doctor] you're welcome. }}<|eot_id|>
