dialogue is: {{ [doctor] jacqueline miller is a 40-year-old female who is being seen today for a follow-up skin exam for perioral dermatitis. she also has a history of rosacea, including ocular rosacea.
[doctor] good morning. how are you today?
[patient] good morning. i'm doing well, thank you.
[doctor] so i see here that you were diagnosed with perioral dermatitis. how are you doing with that? how are you doing with that?
[patient] it's been going on for a while. i've been using the doxycycline cream, and i've been using it twice a day. and it's just getting better. i've been using it twice a day, and it's just getting better. but i was talking to my pharmacist the other day, and she said i should probably go down to once a day.
[doctor] okay. so you were using it twice a day, and then you went down to once a day. and how long have you been using it once a day?
[patient] probably for about a week now.
[doctor] okay. and have you noticed any change in your skin since you went down to once a day?
[patient] no, it's just the same. it's just the same. i mean, it's not worse or anything, it's just not better.
[doctor] okay. and you're also using the sulfacetamide wash, correct?
[patient] yes, i am.
[doctor] and you're using that twice a day, correct?
[patient] yes, i am.
[doctor] okay. and how's that working for you?
[patient] it's really good. my skin's doing really well. i mean, it's not as dry as it was before.
[doctor] okay. and you're also using the metro cream, correct?
[patient] yes, i am.
[doctor] and how's that working for you?
[patient] it's really good. i've been using it twice a day, and it's been really good.
[doctor] okay. and you're not using any other lotions or creams on your skin, correct?
[patient] no, i'm not.
[doctor] okay. and you're not using any other cleansers or anything like that, correct?
[patient] no, i'm not.
[doctor] okay. and you're not using any other products, like neutrogena wipes, or cetaphil lotion or soap, anything like that?
[patient] no, i'm not.
[doctor] okay. so i'm looking at your skin here, and i do see some redness around and underneath your nostrils. and on your cheeks, i see some very faint red spots. so i'm going to call those out. i see some very faint red spots on your cheeks.
[patient] yeah, i've been noticing that.
[doctor] okay. and your skin type is a four to five, correct?
[patient] yeah, that's right.
[doctor] okay. so i'm going to go ahead and order some more doxycycline, and i want you to go back up to twice a day, and i want you to use that until you're all better. and then we're going to go down to once a day for another week, and then you can stop using it.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay? and i'm also going to give you a prescription for tacrolimus, and i want you to apply that to the affected area, and i'm going to give you some instructions on how to use it, and some possible side effects.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay? and i want you to continue using the sulfacetamide wash once a day, and i want you to use the bruder mask as needed.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay? and i want you to continue using the metro cream twice a day.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay? and i want you to gently wash your eyelids once a day with a gentle cleanser, and i want you to try a gentle cream, such as vanicream.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay? and i want you to come back in four weeks, and we're going to check on your skin, and see how you're doing.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay? and if you have any problems, or if your symptoms get worse, please give us a call.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay? and do you have any questions for me?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay. alright, well, it was nice seeing you.
[patient] yeah, same to you.
[doctor] bye-bye.
[patient] bye. }}<|eot_id|>
