dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, how are you?
[patient] i'm doing alright, i'm a little sick. i've been having some abdominal pain.
[doctor] okay, so i see that you're here for some abdominal pain. can you tell me more about what's going on?
[patient] yeah, so i've been having some pain in my right side of my abdomen, and it's been going on for a few days.
[doctor] okay, and have you noticed any other symptoms like nausea or vomiting?
[patient] yeah, i have been feeling a little nauseous and i have thrown up a couple of times.
[doctor] okay, and have you had any fever?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay, and have you had any recent travel or exposure to anyone who's been sick?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay, so let's take a look at your vitals. your blood pressure is 128 over 88, your heart rate is 68, your oxygen saturation is 99 percent on room air, and your temperature is afebrile.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay, so let's do a quick physical exam. your respiratory exam is clear and equal bilaterally. your heart exam is regular rate and rhythm. no ectopic beats, no murmurs, clicks, or rubs. your abdominal exam is soft and non-distended. there is some slight guarding to the right upper quadrant, but without rebound tenderness. there is a positive murphy's sign, but no peritoneal signs appreciated. your bowel sounds are normal in all four quadrants.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay, so based on your symptoms and your exam, it looks like you have some mild cholecystitis with gallstones. i don't appreciate any frank obstruction, but it looks like your gallbladder is inflamed. this appears to have happened maybe over the last few weeks or since you've been symptomatic.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay, so i recommend a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. i've explained the surgery to you. it will take about an hour to an hour and a half. you will probably spend the night in the hospital for post-procedure monitoring. you should avoid high fat foods after surgery and eat foods that are high in fiber.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay, so let's talk a little bit about your diabetes. how are you managing that?
[patient] i'm doing alright. i'm just trying to watch what i eat and get some exercise.
[doctor] okay, and are you seeing an endocrinologist?
[patient] yeah, i am.
[doctor] okay, and do you have any questions about your diabetes?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay, so let's just summarize. you have a laparoscopic cholecystectomy scheduled for next week. you should refrain from hiking in the immediate postoperative period, but you may return to walking on solid ground in about a week.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay, and then for your diabetes, just continue to follow up with your endocrinologist as you have been.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay, do you have any questions about the surgery or anything else?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay, great. well, i'll see you in a week.
[patient] okay, sounds good.
[doctor] okay, bye-bye. }}<|eot_id|>
