dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, matthew. how are you?
[patient] i'm doing alright. how are you?
[doctor] i'm doing well. so, i see here that you were playing football over the weekend and you were tackled a couple of times and landed on your right shoulder. can you tell me a little bit more about that?
[patient] yeah, i was playing football over the weekend and i got tackled a couple of times and i landed on my right shoulder. i was able to get back in the game, but i've had pain since then.
[doctor] okay, and is the pain on the front or the back of your shoulder?
[patient] it's on the back of my shoulder.
[doctor] okay, and have you noticed any numbness or tingling in your fingers?
[patient] no, i haven't.
[doctor] okay, and what about the strength in your hand, is it okay?
[patient] it's okay, but i've been trying to avoid lifting anything too heavy.
[doctor] okay, and can you touch your shoulder to your ear?
[patient] no, i can't.
[doctor] okay, and have you been taking anything for the pain?
[patient] i've been taking tylenol.
[doctor] okay, and has that provided any relief?
[patient] no, it hasn't.
[doctor] okay, and do you have any neck problems or other joint problems?
[patient] no, i don't.
[doctor] okay, and have you had any previous injuries to your right shoulder?
[patient] no, i haven't.
[doctor] okay, and do you have any fever or chills?
[patient] no, i don't have a fever or chills.
[doctor] okay, and did you hit your head when you fell?
[patient] yeah, i hit my head, but i didn't pass out.
[doctor] okay, and what about a headache?
[patient] yeah, i have a headache.
[doctor] okay, so i'm going to go ahead and examine you now. i'm going to examine your neck. does that hurt?
[patient] no, it doesn't.
[doctor] okay, and i'm going to examine your shoulder. does that hurt?
[patient] yeah, that hurts.
[doctor] okay, and i'm going to move your arm around a little bit. does that hurt?
[patient] yeah, that hurts.
[doctor] okay, and i'm going to check your radial artery pulse. is that okay?
[patient] yeah, that's fine.
[doctor] okay, so i'm going to go ahead and look at your x-rays now. and what i see on your x-rays is that you have an acute acromioclavicular joint fracture. so i'm going to explain a little bit about that to you. an acromioclavicular joint fracture is a fracture of the joint that connects your shoulder blade to your collarbone. so what that means is that you've essentially pulled your shoulder apart. and we can treat this with a sling and some anti-inflammatory medication. but what i would like to do is get a ct scan of your shoulder to evaluate that further.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i would also like to get you into physical therapy to strengthen your shoulder in hopes of avoiding surgery in the future. but in the meantime, i would advise you to avoid tackle football. so i'm going to go ahead and write you a prescription for meloxicam 15 milligrams once a day. and i'm going to put you in a sling to help support your shoulder. and i would like you to follow up with me in two weeks to see how you're doing. does that sound like a plan?
[patient] yeah, that sounds good.
[doctor] okay, and do you have any questions for me?
[patient] no, i don't.
[doctor] okay, well, i think that's all for today. i'll have my nurse come in and get you set up with your prescription and your sling. and i'll see you in two weeks.
[patient] okay, sounds good.
[doctor] alright, thank you.
[patient] thank you. }}<|eot_id|>
