dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, teresa, how are you?
[patient] i'm doing okay. i'm in a lot of pain.
[doctor] okay. so i know you called about some right elbow pain. what happened?
[patient] i was doing a lot of pottery with my pottery wheel, and i think i overdid it, and i've been having a lot of pain.
[doctor] okay. so you've been doing a lot of pottery, and you think you overdid it, and now you're having a lot of pain. okay. does it hurt all the time, or is it just when you're doing certain things?
[patient] it hurts all the time.
[doctor] okay. and does it radiate anywhere else?
[patient] it radiates down my arm.
[doctor] okay. and does it hurt when you move your arm or touch it?
[patient] it hurts when i move my arm.
[doctor] okay. and how would you rate your pain, on a scale of one to ten?
[patient] it's a seven.
[doctor] okay. and does it wake you up at night?
[patient] yes, it wakes me up at night.
[doctor] okay. and have you tried anything for the pain?
[patient] no, i've tried ibuprofen, and it doesn't help.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any injuries to your elbow before?
[patient] no, i haven't.
[doctor] okay. and what about any other medical problems? do you have any medical problems?
[patient] i have high blood pressure and diabetes.
[doctor] okay. and how are you managing those?
[patient] i'm taking my medicine, and i've been checking my blood pressures.
[doctor] okay. and what's your blood pressure been running?
[patient] it's been in the one teens.
[doctor] okay. and what medicine are you taking for your blood pressure?
[patient] i'm taking lisinopril.
[doctor] okay. and what about your diabetes? how are you managing that?
[patient] i'm taking metformin, and sometimes i forget to take it.
[doctor] okay. and what's your blood sugars been running?
[patient] it's been running between one fifty to one seventy.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any family history of diabetes?
[patient] no, i don't.
[doctor] okay. and what about any other family history of medical problems?
[patient] no, i don't.
[doctor] okay. and what do you do for exercise?
[patient] i go for a walk in the morning and evening.
[doctor] okay. and how far do you walk?
[patient] about one to two miles a day.
[doctor] okay. and do you like to do any other activities?
[patient] i like to bake cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.
[doctor] okay. that sounds good. and do you have any other hobbies?
[patient] i like to work with ceramics.
[doctor] okay. and do you like to do that a lot?
[patient] yes, i do.
[doctor] okay. well, let's take a look at your blood pressure. your blood pressure is one twenty over seventy. that's good. and your heart rate is good. and your lungs are clear. and your skin looks good. and your muscles and joints look good. but i do appreciate some tenderness over the medial epicondyle of your elbow. and you do have some pain with pronation of your forearm and flexion and bending of your wrist. and you do have some pain with bending and flexion of your elbow. but there's no bruising or swelling. or discoloration or lesions. and i'm going to order an x-ray of your elbow, and i'm going to take a look at that. and i'm going to order a hemoglobin a1c test, and i'm going to increase your metformin to one thousand milligrams twice a day. and i want you to continue to take your lisinopril, ten milligrams a day. and i want you to continue to exercise. i want you to wear a sling on your right arm while you're awake. and i want you to ice your elbow for twenty minutes three times a day. and i want you to take ibuprofen six hundred milligrams every six hours with food for one week. and i want you to hold off on creating pottery for the next two weeks. and if you continue to have pain, we might have to consider formal physical therapy. and i want you to check and record your blood sugars every day. and i want you to see a diabetic counselor for more information on steps you can take to manage your diabetes. and i want you to continue with your walking routine. so i want you to follow up with me in two weeks to review the results of your hemoglobin a1c test and discuss your progress. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] all right, thank you.
[patient] thank you. }}<|eot_id|>
