dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, how are you?
[patient] i'm doing alright. how are you?
[doctor] i'm doing alright. so i know you're here for a follow up. so you know you had a heart attack about six months ago, right?
[patient] yes, that's correct.
[doctor] and you had a stent placed in your left anterior descending artery, correct?
[patient] yes.
[doctor] okay. and you also have a history of diabetes and coronary artery disease. how are you doing with that?
[patient] i'm doing alright. i'm taking my medications and trying to stay active.
[doctor] okay. and you're taking the aspirin, brilinta, fish oil, crestor, and metoprolol, correct?
[patient] yes, that's correct.
[doctor] okay. and how are you feeling?
[patient] i'm feeling good.
[doctor] okay. and any dizziness or headaches?
[patient] yeah, i get a little dizzy when i stand up quick.
[doctor] okay. and any muscle aches?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and any depression or anxiety?
[patient] yeah, i've been feeling a little down since the heart attack.
[doctor] okay. and any suicidal or homicidal thoughts?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. good. so let's go ahead and take a look at your vital signs. so your blood pressure is looking good, 127 over 80. and your heart rate is 64. and your oxygenation is 98 percent on room air.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. so let's go ahead and do a quick physical exam. so on your neck exam, your neck is supple without jugular venous distention. and on your heart exam, you do have a slight, 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base. and on your lung exam, your lungs are clear bilaterally. and on your lower extremity exam, you do have one plus non-pitting edema.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. and i'm going to go ahead and review some of your results. so your labs are looking good. your creatinine level is stable. and i'm going to go ahead and order another one just to make sure.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. so let's go ahead and talk about my assessment and plan. so for your first problem of your coronary artery disease, you're doing very well. you're very compliant with your medications. you do have that slight ejection murmur that we heard on exam, but that's stable. and you're experiencing some edema, but that seems to be medication related. so i'm going to go ahead and prescribe you a refill on your metoprolol, 50 milligrams once a day, and your crestor, 40 milligrams once a day. and i'm also going to go ahead and refer you to cardiac rehab to help you get more active and confident with exercising.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i want you to watch for any symptoms such as chest pain or shortness of breath on exertion. if you have those symptoms, we'll need to do some further testing such as a stress test.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. for your second problem of your stage three chronic kidney disease, your most recent creatinine levels are stable. i'm going to go ahead and order another one just to make sure. but i want you to follow up with your nephrologist, dr. miller.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. for your third problem of your diabetes, you know your most recent hemoglobin a1c was 16. i want you to try to get that down to six. so i'm going to go ahead and order another hemoglobin a1c today. and i'm going to go ahead and refer you to a dietitian for some diabetes education. and i'm also going to go ahead and contact your primary care physician to discuss your insulin regimen.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. and for your fourth problem of your mild depression, i want you to follow up with a psychiatrist.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. so do you have any questions or concerns?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay. well, we'll go ahead and get you checked out. and we'll go ahead and get you scheduled for those appointments that we discussed.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. great. thanks.
[patient] thanks. }}<|eot_id|>
