dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hey, christopher watson is an eighty six year old male who returns in follow up of adult hydrocephalus. this is his first visit since undergoing shunt surgery on august first, two thousand twenty. he is accompanied by his daughter, theresa, together they give his history.
[doctor] so, christopher, tell me a little bit about how you've been doing.
[patient] well, i'm doing good. i'm doing good. i'm just trying to stay out of trouble. you know, just trying to stay out of trouble.
[doctor] all right. well, i'm glad to hear that. so, i know that you had some issues with your gait, your bladder control, and your cognitive function. and you had a shunt put in. how are you doing with those things?
[patient] well, i'm doing better. i'm doing better. i can walk to the dining room, which i couldn't do before. i'm still having a little trouble walking, but i'm doing better.
[doctor] okay. and how about your bladder control? are you having to wear a diaper?
[patient] yes, i'm wearing a diaper. i'm wearing a diaper.
[doctor] okay. and how about your thinking? are you able to do things that you were having trouble with before?
[patient] yes, i'm able to do things that i was having trouble with before. i'm able to have a conversation with my daughter, which i couldn't do before.
[doctor] okay. that's great to hear. and how about your headaches? are you having any headaches?
[patient] no, i'm not having any headaches.
[doctor] okay. and how about any pain in your abdomen or your back?
[patient] no, i'm not having any pain.
[doctor] okay. all right. well, let's talk a little bit about your past medical history. do you have any other conditions that we should be aware of?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any allergies to any medications?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay. all right. well, let's talk a little bit about your review of systems. are you having any fever or unintentional weight loss?
[patient] no, i'm not having any fever or unintentional weight loss.
[doctor] okay. are you having any urinary issues?
[patient] yes, i'm having urinary issues.
[doctor] okay. are you having any problems with your memory or concentration?
[patient] no, i'm not having any problems with my memory or concentration.
[doctor] okay. are you having any problems with your balance or walking?
[patient] yes, i'm having problems with my balance and walking.
[doctor] okay. are you having any problems with your mood or depression?
[patient] no, i'm not having any problems with my mood or depression.
[doctor] okay. all right. well, let's talk a little bit about your vital signs. your blood pressure is one twenty four over eighty. your heart rate is sixty four. your respiratory rate is eighteen. your weight is one seventy four pounds. and you're not having any pain.
[doctor] okay. all right. well, let's talk a little bit about your physical exam. i'm going to go ahead and do a physical exam on you. your heart is normal. your lungs are clear. your abdomen is soft. your neuro exam is normal. your gait is slow. your balance is a little bit impaired. and your cranial nerves are intact.
[doctor] okay. all right. well, let's talk a little bit about the results of your tests. your ct scan from october fifteenth, two thousand twenty shows a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of munro of four point six centimeters with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of ten millimeters. by my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with just a tiny subdural collection. this was not noticed by the radiologist who stated no extra axial fluid collections. there is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.
[doctor] okay. well, let's talk a little bit about your assessment. i think that you're doing well. you've made some improvement with your hydrocephalus since your shunt surgery. i do think that you have a tiny fluid collection in the right parietal region. i do not believe that this tiny amount of fluid is symptomatic, and it was not documented by the radiologist when he read the ct scan. i do think that you've made some improvement with your gait. i think that you're doing well with your bladder control. and i think that you've made some improvement with your cognitive function.
[doctor] okay. well, let's talk a little bit about your plan. i think that we should leave the setting at two point zero for another three months before we consider changing the shunt. i think that you should follow up with me in three months.
[doctor] okay. any questions?
[patient] yes. can i drive?
[doctor] well, i think that it's unlikely that you'll be able to drive again. i know that you're doing a little better, but i think that you're still having some trouble with your speed of movement. i know that you're doing a little better with your gait, but i think that you're still having some trouble with your speed of movement. i think that it's unlikely that you'll be able to pass a driver's safety evaluation. however, occasionally patients surprise me by improving enough over nine to twelve months that they are able to pass such a test. i would certainly be happy to recommend such a test if i believe you are likely to pass it. however, i think it is quite likely you will not regain sufficient speed of motion to pass such a test.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. any other questions?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. well, i'm glad to see you, christopher. i'm glad to see you're doing well. i'll see you in three months.
[patient] okay. }}<|eot_id|>
