dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, amanda how are you?
[patient] hi, i'm doing okay.
[doctor] so, i see you were here last time for your hypertension, and we discussed that you were taking hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 milligrams once daily and lisinopril 20 milligrams once daily. and your blood pressure was running a little high, so i added the lisinopril. how are you doing with that?
[patient] i'm doing okay. i'm taking the medication.
[doctor] okay, good. so, i know you had a little trouble with the lisinopril, though, with some headaches and a little bit of a, you know, what you called a "swooshing" in your left ear. and some lethargy. did you continue taking it?
[patient] yes, i did. i took half a dose.
[doctor] okay, good. and did you continue to experience those side effects?
[patient] no, not really.
[doctor] okay. and how about your blood pressure? how's that been?
[patient] it's been running a little high. it's averaging 135 to 145 over 95 to 110.
[doctor] okay. and are you checking it in the morning and evening?
[patient] yes, i am.
[doctor] okay. and do you notice any difference, you know, when you're sitting or standing?
[patient] it's higher at night. but after sitting for a few minutes, it goes down.
[doctor] okay. well, that's good. so, i know you have a history of alcohol use disorder. how's that going?
[patient] i'm still drinking.
[doctor] okay. and how often do you drink?
[patient] four to five times a week.
[doctor] okay. and do you think that's a problem?
[patient] i do. i do.
[doctor] okay. well, you know, i think your blood pressure would likely improve if you stopped drinking. and i'm going to offer you naltrexone injections. have you heard of that?
[patient] yes, i have.
[doctor] okay. and what are your thoughts about it?
[patient] i'm interested.
[doctor] okay. well, let me tell you a little bit about it. it's an injectable medication that we use for alcohol use disorder. and it helps to block the high you get from drinking, so you don't get that feeling of being high, and that can help you to cut back. and we'll start you on it next week, on may seventeenth. and i'll give you the first injection then. and we'll see you back in a few weeks and give you the second injection, and then we'll re-evaluate you at that time. does that sound okay?
[patient] okay, that sounds good.
[doctor] okay, good. and are you willing to commit to that?
[patient] yes, i am.
[doctor] okay, good. and are you aware of the potential side effects of the medication?
[patient] i'm aware.
[doctor] okay. well, we'll discuss those when we give you the medication. and are you willing to stop drinking for the duration of the medication?
[patient] yes, i am.
[doctor] okay, good. and i know you have a history of hypertension, and we've been managing that with the lisinopril and the hydrochlorothiazide. how's your blood pressure been?
[patient] it's been running a little high.
[doctor] okay. well, we'll continue the lisinopril at 10 milligrams a day and the hydrochlorothiazide at 12.5 milligrams a day. and i want to see you back in a few weeks, let's say the week of june fourteenth, to check on you. and i'll give you that second injection of the naltrexone then. and we'll see how you're doing. does that sound okay?
[patient] okay, that sounds good.
[doctor] okay, good. and do you have any other questions for me?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay, good. well, let's do a quick physical exam on you. so, your blood pressure is a little high. your heart is regular, you don't have any extra heart sounds. your lungs are clear. your skin is good, no swelling in your ankles. and your neurological exam is normal. do you have any other questions?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay, good. well, i'm going to go ahead and prescribe you the naltrexone. we'll get you started on that. and i'll see you back in a few weeks. okay?
[patient] okay, that sounds good.
[doctor] okay, good. and do you have any other questions for me?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay, good. well, let's get you checked out. }}<|eot_id|>
