[doctor] okay, i'm going to do a quick examination of your hand. i'm just going to press on your hand here. does that hurt?
[patient] no, it does not.
[doctor] okay, i'm going to check your radial artery pulse. does that hurt?
[patient] no, it does not.
[doctor] okay, let's take a look at your x-ray. your x-ray of your right shoulder shows an acute acromioclavicular joint fracture. i'm going to explain what that means to you. it means that you have a break in the bone in your shoulder. it's a pretty common injury, especially for football players. i'm going to put you in a sling to help reduce your pain and swelling. i'm also going to prescribe meloxicam 15 milligrams once a day to help with your pain. i'm also going to recommend that we get a ct scan of your right shoulder to evaluate further. i'm also going to recommend that you attend formal physical therapy to strengthen your right shoulder in hopes of avoiding surgery in the future. in the meantime, i would advise you to avoid playing tackle football. do you have any questions?
[patient] no, i do not.
[doctor] okay, well, let's get you checked out and get you on the road to recovery. i'll have my nurse come in and get you set up with the sling and the medication. i'll also have my nurse order that ct scan for you. do you have any other questions for me?
