dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, teresa. how are you?
[patient] i'm doing okay.
[doctor] good. so i know the nurse told you about dax. i'd like to tell dax a little bit about you. so, teresa is a 33-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. her pain began four days ago, without any improvement. she denies any specific injury ; however, she has recently increased her usage of a pottery wheel in creating a bowl for her son. she is currently unable to work with the pottery wheel secondary to pain. her pain is in the medial aspect of her elbow. she notes it radiates medially down her arm as well. the patient rates her pain level as seven out of ten at its worst. she reports the pain wakes her at night if she rolls over. she denies ibuprofen or ice provide pain relief. the patient denies any previous elbow injuries.
[doctor] okay. so, teresa, i know you've been making these bowls for your son. how's that going?
[patient] yeah, i've been making some bowls. i'm just, i'm really proud of them.
[doctor] that's great. i'm sure your son is going to love them.
[patient] yeah, he's really excited.
[doctor] good. okay. so, it sounds like you're making some bowls.
[patient] yeah.
[doctor] okay. so, teresa, i know that you have a history of hypertension. how have you been doing with that?
[patient] yeah, i've been checking my blood pressures regularly. they've been in the one-ten-ones to one-three-thirties. so, they're pretty good.
[doctor] okay. and you're still on the lisinopril, ten milligrams a day?
[patient] yes, i am.
[doctor] okay. and have you been missing any of those?
[patient] no, i have not.
[doctor] okay. and how about your diabetes? how are you doing with that?
[patient] yeah, i sometimes forget to take my metformin, but i'm trying to be better about it.
[doctor] okay. so, you're taking the five hundred milligrams twice a day?
[patient] yeah, i am.
[doctor] okay. and have you been checking your sugars regularly?
[patient] yeah, i try to.
[doctor] okay. and what's been your blood sugar running?
[patient] it's been running, you know, it's been running anywhere from one-fifty to one-seventy.
[doctor] okay. okay. so, let's talk a little bit about your pain. so, you said that you've been making these bowls for your son. and you've been doing that for a while?
[patient] yeah, i've been doing it for a while. i've been making pottery for years.
[doctor] okay. okay. and you said that you've been making a lot of bowls for your son, which is great. but, have you been doing anything else with the pottery wheel?
[patient] yeah, i've been doing a lot of different things with it.
[doctor] okay. so, have you been making anything else?
[patient] yeah, i've been making some plates and some mugs.
[doctor] okay. okay. so, let's talk a little bit about your pain. so, you said that it's in the medial aspect of your elbow. does it radiate anywhere else?
[patient] yeah, it goes down my arm.
[doctor] okay. okay. and does it hurt when you move your arm in any way?
[patient] yeah, it hurts when i bend it.
[doctor] okay. okay. all right. well, i'm gon na go ahead and examine you. i'm gon na take a look at your elbow. so, i'm gon na press on this. does that hurt?
[patient] yeah, it hurts a lot.
[doctor] okay. okay. all right. and then i'm gon na bend your wrist and see if that hurts.
[patient] yeah, it hurts a lot.
[doctor] okay. all right. so, let me look at this. so, i'm just gon na look at your elbow. and, i'm gon na move it around a little bit. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. all right. so, let's talk a little bit about your results. so, we did an x-ray of your elbow. and, it looks like it's negative for any fractures or any other abnormalities.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. so, i'm gon na go ahead and tell you a little bit about my assessment and my plan. so, i think that you have what we call medial epicondylitis. this is an overuse injury. it's a strain of the tendons in your elbow. and, i think that this is why you're having the pain.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. so, what i'm gon na do is, i'm gon na put you in a sling. i want you to wear it while you're awake. i want you to ice your elbow for twenty minutes three times a day. i want you to take some ibuprofen six hundred milligrams every six hours with food. i want you to do that for about a week. and, i want you to hold off on creating pottery for the next couple of weeks to allow your elbow to heal.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. and, i'm gon na increase your metformin to a thousand milligrams twice a day. i'm gon na have you come back in two weeks to review the results of your hemoglobin a1c test. and, we're gon na go from there.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. and, i'm also gon na refer you to some diabetic counseling. i think that would be helpful for you to learn more about managing your diabetes.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. and, i want you to continue with your walking routine. and, continue taking your lisinopril.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. and, i want you to check your sugars every day. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. all right. well, it was great seeing you today.
[patient] thank you.
[doctor] you're welcome. take care. }}<|eot_id|>
