dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, alexis. how are you?
[patient] hi. i'm okay.
[doctor] good. so, i understand that you're here today for some pain in your right hand. what's going on?
[patient] yeah. so, i've been having some numbness and tingling in my right hand. and it's been going on for a while now.
[doctor] okay. and how long has that been going on?
[patient] um, it started about six months ago. and it's been getting worse.
[doctor] okay. and what's happening in your right hand?
[patient] so, it's mostly in my first, second, and third fingers. and it's numbness and tingling. and it's constant now. it's not intermittent anymore.
[doctor] okay. and what have you been doing that's causing this?
[patient] um, i'm on the computer all day. i work on a keyboard and mouse.
[doctor] okay. and does it bother you when you're sleeping?
[patient] no, not really.
[doctor] okay. and what have you been doing for the pain?
[patient] um, i've been taking ibuprofen and resting my hand.
[doctor] okay. and has that been helping?
[patient] it's been helping a little bit.
[doctor] okay. and how about any other symptoms, like pain, swelling, redness?
[patient] no, nothing like that.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any problems like this before?
[patient] no, nothing like this.
[doctor] okay. let me just take a look at your hand. i'm just gon na check your right hand. i'm gon na move your fingers. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. and i'm gon na press on your wrist. okay?
[patient] mm-hmm.
[doctor] okay. and i'm gon na check your grip strength. okay?
[patient] mm-hmm.
[doctor] okay. and your grip strength on the right is less than on the left. and when i press on your wrist, i can reproduce your numbness and tingling. so, i'm gon na do one more thing. i'm gon na tap on the back of your wrist here. okay?
[patient] mm-hmm.
[doctor] okay. and when i do that, i can reproduce your numbness and tingling. so, i'm gon na call that a positive tinnel's sign.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. so, based on your symptoms and my exam, it sounds like you have carpal tunnel syndrome.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] so, what does that mean?
[patient] what does it mean?
[doctor] well, it means that there's compression of the nerve that runs down your arm and into your hand. it's like a tight tunnel. and that's causing the numbness and tingling in your fingers.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i think we can treat this conservatively. i want you to wear a wrist splint at night. and i want you to take ibuprofen, but i want you to take 600 milligrams, four times a day. and that should help with the pain and the swelling.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i'm gon na order an emg/nerve conduction study to see if that shows any compression of the nerve.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and if the emg shows that the nerve is compressed, we can consider physical therapy or surgery to release the nerve.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay? any questions?
[patient] no, that's it.
[doctor] okay. i'll get the nurse in here and we'll get you set up for that nerve test. okay?
[patient] okay. thank you.
[doctor] you're welcome. }}<|eot_id|>
