dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, miss, how are you?
[patient] i'm doing okay, how are you?
[doctor] i'm doing well, thank you. so, i know you're here for a follow-up status post inferior stemi, so i'll go ahead and review your history. so, you had a heart attack in 2018 and we put a stent in your right coronary artery. and since then, you've been doing okay. you're on aspirin 81 milligrams daily, brilinta 90 milligrams twice daily, lipitor 80 milligrams daily, toprol 50 milligrams daily, lisinopril 20 milligrams daily, and metformin. have you had any chest pain?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay, any shortness of breath?
[patient] no.
[doctor] any swelling in your legs?
[patient] yeah, yeah, my legs are swollen.
[doctor] okay. and your blood pressure is well controlled. let's go ahead and do a quick physical exam. your vital signs are all within normal limits. on your neck exam, there is no jugular venous distention. on your heart exam, there is a 3 over 6 systolic ejection murmur at the left base. your radial artery cath site is clean, dry, and intact without hematoma. radial artery pulses are palpable. on your lung exam, your lungs are clear bilaterally. and on your lower extremity exam, there is bilateral lower limb edema. pitting one plus. so, let's look at some results. your ekg is normal. good r wave progression. evolutionary changes that are anticipated after a stemi. and your echocardiogram shows a reduced ejection fraction of 35 percent. so, let's talk about your assessment and plan. first, you have a history of coronary artery disease, and we'll continue to monitor that. you will continue aspirin 81 milligrams daily and brilinta 90 milligrams twice daily. you will continue lipitor 80 milligrams a day and toprol 50 milligrams daily. i'm going to refer you to cardiac rehab for education and to gain confidence to resume exercising regularly. second, you have newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation. a recent echocardiogram revealed a reduced ejection fraction of 35 percent. you were able to be treated in the cath lab in a timely manner, so i think that the muscle is just stunned. given your history, it will improve over time. i advised you that you will recover since you are compliant with your medications. continue lisinopril 20 milligrams daily. third, you have acute heart failure. you are retaining fluid. i'm going to prescribe lasix 40 milligrams once a day. i want to add aldactone 12.5 milligrams daily. i will order lab work. we will obtain a repeat echo in approximately two months. fourth, you have hypertension. your blood pressure is well controlled. you will tolerate the aldactone well. i encouraged you to continue exercising and to be cognizant of your diet. i explained that there are healthier alternatives available to reduce the amount of preserved meats you consume. continue lisinopril 20 milligrams daily. the patient received a referral to nutrition counseling to consider what other diet options might be available to her. and fifth, you have type 2 diabetes. it is stable. i encouraged you to continue monitoring your blood glucose levels. continue metformin as prescribed. any questions?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay, great. the patient will follow up as instructed. }}<|eot_id|>
