dialogue is: {{ [doctor] good morning, ms. thompson. how are you doing today?
[patient] good morning, doctor. i'm doing okay. i've been having some knee pain. i'm not sure what's going on with it.
[doctor] okay. what's going on with your knee? how did you hurt it?
[patient] i've been swimming more and trying to get more active and i've been doing some laps in the pool and it starts hurting after a couple of laps. i've been able to do like ten laps before, but now it's been doing this for a couple of weeks. i haven't hurt it or anything. i don't know what's going on.
[doctor] okay. have you fallen or hurt your knee or anything like that?
[patient] no, nothing like that. i just think i might be doing too much.
[doctor] okay. and how about your back? how's your back doing?
[patient] my back's been doing pretty well. it's been doing okay. it's been a little bit sore, but it's been okay.
[doctor] okay. and how about your depression? how's that going?
[patient] that's been going really well. i've been getting out more and that's been helping me with my depression. i've been sleeping well and eating well, so that's good.
[doctor] okay, good. and have you had any suicidal thoughts or any concerns about hurting yourself or others?
[patient] no, nothing like that.
[doctor] okay. and how's your hand doing? how's your carpal tunnel doing?
[patient] that's been doing really well. i've been getting full range of motion and it's felt a lot better.
[doctor] okay. and are you doing any exercises for that or anything like that?
[patient] i've been doing some exercises for that.
[doctor] okay. good. well, i'm gon na go ahead and do a quick physical exam on you. i'm gon na call out some of my findings as i go along. so, on physical exam, your vital signs look good. your heart rate and blood pressure are good. on your neurological exam, your mood and affect look good. your grip strength is equal bilaterally. on your neck exam, your neck is supple without any thyromegaly or cervical lymphadenopathy. your lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. there are no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi. on your heart exam, your heart rate and rhythm are regular. there are no murmurs, gallops, or rubs. there are no extra heart sounds. on your musculoskeletal exam, there is no lower extremity edema. there is pain with patellar inhibition. there is pain with range of motion of your right knee. so, i did order an x-ray of your right knee. the x-ray looks unremarkable. it doesn't show any fractures or any bony abnormalities. so, for your knee pain, i think you might have some chondromalacia of your patella. that means that your cartilage is a little bit inflamed in the front of your knee. i'm gon na prescribe you some ibuprofen, six hundred milligrams four times a day with food. i want you to do some straight leg raising exercises that will strengthen your quadriceps. i also want you to try to avoid any heavy activity that might strain your knee, like running or jumping. maybe try swimming some freestyle laps or just stop activity altogether. if your pain doesn't improve in a couple of weeks, we'll follow up. for your back, it's been stable and well-controlled. just continue swimming regularly once your knee is feeling better. for your depression, you're doing well with your current regimen. just continue with your current medications. for your carpal tunnel, it's been stable status post your carpal tunnel release. just continue with your current regimen with no changes at this time. any questions?
[patient] no, that sounds good.
[doctor] okay. i'll have my nurse come in and get you set up with your prescriptions and your appointment for the next time. sounds good?
[patient] sounds good. thank you.
[doctor] okay. you're welcome. i'll talk to you later.
[patient] okay, bye.
[doctor] bye. }}<|eot_id|>
