dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, mr. watson, how are you doing?
[patient] i'm doing okay. i'm doing okay.
[doctor] okay. so, i'm gon na be calling you mr. watson. so, i know we've seen you in the past for your adult hydrocephalus. i know you had a shunt surgery, so i'm gon na tell you a little bit about my findings and then we'll talk about my impression and my plan.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] so, i know you had a shunt surgery in august. and since then, you've been doing well. and i know you're here today because you're here for a follow-up. so, how are you doing? are you able to walk around? are you able to do things that you were having trouble doing before?
[patient] i'm able to walk to the dining room, but i wasn't able to do that before the surgery.
[doctor] okay. so, that's good. i'm glad to hear that. and are you able to walk without any assistance?
[patient] i can walk without assistance.
[doctor] okay. and how about your balance? are you able to balance okay?
[patient] my balance has improved, but i still have some walking impairment.
[doctor] okay. and what about your bladder control? are you able to go to the bathroom okay?
[patient] initially, there was some improvement, but i've kind of leveled off, and i'm still wearing a diaper.
[doctor] okay. and are you having any headaches or pain at the shunt site or in your abdomen?
[patient] no headaches and no pain.
[doctor] okay. so, i know you had a shunt surgery, and i know you were having some trouble with your walking and your balance and your bladder control. and so, i'm gon na be checking your vitals today. so, let me just check your vitals. your blood pressure is 124 over 80. your heart rate is 64. your respiratory rate is 18. your weight is 174 pounds. and your pain is zero out of 10. so, that's all looking good. and then i'm gon na do a physical exam. so, on physical exam, your shunt site is clean and dry and intact. and then i'm gon na check your neurological exam. so, on neurological exam, your mental status, i tested you for recent and remote memory. your attention span and concentration are all intact. your fund of knowledge is good. you scored 26 out of 30 on the mmse when you were tested with spelling, and 25 out of 30 when you were tested with calculations. and that's a slight improvement from your initial score of 23 out of 30 with calculations and 24 out of 30 with spelling and at that time, you were unable to remember any memory words with cuing and only one with multiple choice. so, that's good. your gait, i tested you with the tinetti assessment tool. you were tested without an assistive device. you received a gait score of 6 to 8 out of 12 and a balance of score of 12 out of 16 for a total score of 18 to 20 out of 28. and that's a slight improvement from your initial score of 15 to 17 out of 28. and then, on cranial nerves, your pupils are equal. your extraocular movements are intact. your face is symmetric. no dysarthria. and then, on motor exam, your bulk and strength are normal. your coordination is slow for finger-to-nose. so, i know you had a ct scan of your head in october. and let me just show you the results. so, the ct scan shows that there is a frontal horn span at the level of foramen of munro of 4.6 centimeters with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 millimeters. by my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with just a tiny subdural collection. this was not noticed by the radiologist, who stated no extra axial fluid collections. and there is also substantial small vessel ischemic change. so, i know you had some improvement with your hydrocephalus since the shunt surgery. and so, i think we should leave the setting at 2.0 for another three months before we consider changing the shunt. and then, i know you asked me if you'll be able to drive again. unfortunately, i think it's unlikely that your speed of movement will improve to a level that you'll be able to pass a driver's safety evaluation, however, occasionally patients surprise me by improving enough over nine to 12 months that they're able to pass such a test. so, i would certainly be happy to recommend such a test if i believe you're likely to pass it. and you're always welcome to enroll in a driver's safety program without my recommendation, however, i think it's exceeding unlikely that you have the capability of passing this rigorous test at this time. and i also think it's quite likely you will not regain sufficient speed of motion to pass such a test. so, i think that's all for today. do you have any questions for me?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay. so, i'm gon na have you follow up in three months. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] all right, thank you.
[patient] thank you.
[doctor] okay, bye-bye. }}<|eot_id|>
