dialogue is: {{ [doctor] jacqueline miller is a 40-year-old female who is being seen today for a follow-up skin exam for perioral dermatitis. she also has a history of rosacea, including ocular rosacea. so, jacqueline, how are you doing today?
[patient] i'm doing alright. i'm doing alright.
[doctor] okay. so, i know that you've had some issues with the perioral dermatitis in the past. and i know that we've had you on the doxycycline. how's that been going?
[patient] it's been okay. it's been okay. i've been using it twice a day, but then my pharmacist told me to cut back to once a day. so, for the past week, i've been using it once a day. and i haven't really seen any more improvement, but it hasn't gotten any worse.
[doctor] okay. and are you using the sulfacetamide wash?
[patient] yes, i am. i use it twice a day.
[doctor] okay. and do you notice any drying of your skin?
[patient] no, i don't.
[doctor] okay. and do you use any other lotions or products on your face?
[patient] no, i don't.
[doctor] okay. and have you started the elidel?
[patient] no, i haven't. it's not covered by my insurance, and i was a little concerned about the side effects.
[doctor] okay. all right. so, i'm gon na go ahead and do a quick physical exam. okay? so, on your skin exam today, you have a few very faint, erythematous papules on both sides of your cheeks. and there is some mild redness around and underneath your nostrils. your skin type is about a four to five out of six. okay? so, for your perioral dermatitis, i think we've made some progress, but it's not fully resolved. and i think we can try to push it a little bit further. so, i'm gon na recommend that you increase the doxycycline to twice a day. but we'll go ahead and do that for a couple of weeks, and then you can try to cut back to once a day. and then we can see how you do. and i'm also gon na recommend that you continue to use the sulfacetamide wash once a day. and i'm gon na give you a prescription for tacrolimus, okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i want to talk a little bit about the tacrolimus. it's a topical cream, and it's gon na help with the inflammation in your skin. but it can have some side effects, like itching or burning. and it can also make your skin more sensitive to the sun, okay? so, i want to make sure you understand all of that before we start.
[patient] okay, i understand.
[doctor] okay. and then for your rosacea, including the ocular rosacea, i want you to continue using the metrocream twice a day. and i also want you to start washing your eyelids once a day with a gentle cleanser, okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i'm gon na give you a prescription for vanicream, which is a gentle cream that you can use on your eyelids, okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. so, do you have any questions for me?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay. all right. i'll have my nurse come in and give you the prescriptions. and we'll have you come back in four weeks, okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] all right. and if you have any problems, don't hesitate to give us a call.
[patient] okay. thank you.
[doctor] you're welcome. }}<|eot_id|>
