dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, matthew. how are you?
[patient] i'm doing okay. i've been in a lot of pain since i hurt my shoulder.
[doctor] okay. so, i understand that you hurt your shoulder playing football. what happened?
[patient] i was playing football and i got tackled a couple of times and i landed on my right shoulder. and i was able to get back up and continue playing, but i've been having pain ever since.
[doctor] okay. so, when you say you landed on your shoulder, where exactly on your shoulder?
[patient] it was the back of my shoulder.
[doctor] okay. and did you get your head hit at all?
[patient] i did hit my head, but i didn't pass out or anything.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any numbness or tingling in your fingers?
[patient] no, i haven't.
[doctor] okay. and how about lifting your arm up, can you touch your shoulder to your ear?
[patient] no, i cann't. it's really painful.
[doctor] okay. and what about your neck, any pain there?
[patient] no, i don't have any neck pain.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any other injuries to your shoulder in the past?
[patient] no, not that i can think of.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any fever or chills since the injury?
[patient] no, i haven't had any fever or chills.
[doctor] okay. and what have you been taking for pain?
[patient] i've been taking tylenol.
[doctor] okay. and does that help?
[patient] no, it doesn't really.
[doctor] okay. and how long ago did you get hurt?
[patient] it was four days ago.
[doctor] okay. and have you been able to do anything that makes the pain worse?
[patient] i've been trying to avoid lifting anything heavy.
[doctor] okay. and have you been able to do anything that makes the pain better?
[patient] not really.
[doctor] okay. all right. well, i'm gon na go ahead and do a physical exam on you. i'm gon na check your neck and your shoulder. so, looking at your neck, there is no tenderness to palpation. full range of motion. and looking at your shoulder, there is pain to palpation of the back of your shoulder, at the acromioclavicular joint. you have decreased abduction and adduction to about 90 degrees only. and there is pain with supination and pronation of your right arm. your radial artery pulse is palpable. your blood pressure is 122 over 76. your heart rate is 82. your oxygen saturation is 95 % on room air. all right, i'm gon na go ahead and order an x-ray of your right shoulder. and i'm gon na order a ct of your shoulder as well. and i'm gon na prescribe you meloxicam 15 milligrams once a day for your pain. and i'm gon na recommend that you attend formal physical therapy to strengthen your right shoulder, hopefully to avoid surgery in the future. and i want you to avoid tackle football. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. so, i will have my nurse come in and get you set up with those prescriptions and the physical therapy. and i will have the radiology department call you with the results of your x-ray and ct. and if you have any questions or concerns before then, just give us a call. okay?
[patient] okay, sounds good. thank you.
[doctor] you're welcome. i'll talk to you soon. }}<|eot_id|>
