dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hey, how are you doing?
[patient] i'm doing alright. i'm just having a hard time adjusting to all the new medications and stuff.
[doctor] i understand. it's a lot to take in. so, let's go ahead and get started. i reviewed your medical history. i see that you had a heart attack about six months ago. you've had coronary artery disease and diabetes. you also had a stent placed in the left anterior descending artery. i'm gon na go ahead and review your medications. you're taking aspirin, brilinta, fish oil, crestor, and metoprolol 50 milligrams once a day. is that correct?
[patient] yes, that's correct.
[doctor] okay. let's go ahead and do a quick review of systems. how are you doing? any muscle aches or anything like that?
[patient] no, i don't have any muscle aches. i do have some non-pitting edema in my lower extremities.
[doctor] okay. any dizziness or headaches?
[patient] yeah, i do have some dizziness and headaches.
[doctor] okay. and how about depression? how are you doing with that?
[patient] yeah, i've been having a hard time with depression.
[doctor] okay. and are you feeling suicidal or homicidal at all?
[patient] no, i don't have any suicidal or homicidal ideation.
[doctor] okay. great. let's go ahead and take a look at your vital signs. your blood pressure is 127 over 80. that's really good. now, let's do a quick physical exam. on your neck exam, i don't see any jugular venous distention or carotid bruits. on your respiratory exam, your respiratory effort is normal. your lungs are clear bilaterally. i don't hear any wheezes, rales, or rhonchi. on your cardiovascular exam, i do hear a slight, three out of six systolic ejection murmur at the left base. on your musculoskeletal exam, i do see one plus non-pitting edema in your lower extremities. let's go ahead and look at some of your lab results. your creatinine level is stable. now, let's talk about my assessment and plan. for your coronary artery disease, you're doing really well. you're very compliant with your medications, and i think it's great. i do hear that three out of six systolic ejection murmur, but that's something that we've heard before, and it's not concerning. you're experiencing some non-pitting edema, but that's probably related to your medications. i do think it's very common for people to experience dizziness when they stand up quickly, so we'll just have to work on that. i think it's great that you're active, and i think that cardiac rehab will be beneficial in helping you become more active and confident with exercising. i do want you to watch for any symptoms like chest pain or shortness of breath on exertion, because that would need further testing such as a stress test. for your stage three chronic kidney disease, your most recent creatinine levels are stable, so that's great. i do think it's very important that you follow up with nephrology. for your diabetes, i know that you had a hemoglobin a1c of 16. i think it's very important that we manage your diabetes because it plays a big role in your coronary artery disease. your goal for your hemoglobin a1c will be six. i do think it's very important that you work on managing your diabetes. i've ordered another hemoglobin a1c, and i'm also going to go ahead and provide you with a dietitian referral for diabetes education. we'll also contact your primary care physician regarding your insulin regimen. for your mild depression, i do think it's very common for people to feel depressed after a heart attack, and i think it's great that you're reaching out for help. i'm going to go ahead and provide you with a referral to psychiatry. i do think it's very important that you work on managing your depression. i want you to understand and agree with the recommended medical treatment plan.
