dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, alexis. how are you?
[patient] i'm doing okay. i'm having some pain in my hand.
[doctor] okay. so, um, how long have you been having this pain? what's going on?
[patient] so, about six months ago, i started noticing some numbness and tingling in my first, second, and third fingers on my right hand. and over the last month or so, it's just been constant. and it's really bothers me when i'm using the mouse or typing on the keyboard.
[doctor] okay. and does it bother you at night? do you wake up with it?
[patient] no, it's more during the day, um, when i'm using the computer.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any trouble sleeping because of it?
[patient] no, i haven't.
[doctor] okay. um, and what have you tried to help with the symptoms?
[patient] um, i've just been resting my hand and taking some ibuprofen.
[doctor] okay. and has that helped at all?
[patient] yeah, it helps a little bit.
[doctor] okay. and can you tell me a little bit about what you do? what kind of work do you do?
[patient] yeah, i work on a keyboard all day.
[doctor] okay. okay. um, and what kind of job is it?
[patient] i'm an administrative assistant.
[doctor] okay. okay. and how many hours a day are you on the computer?
[patient] um, probably about 8 hours a day.
[doctor] okay. um, okay. and have you noticed any other symptoms? like weakness in your hand or anything like that?
[patient] no, nothing like that.
[doctor] okay. um, and have you had any other medical problems in the past?
[patient] no, nothing that i can think of.
[doctor] okay. um, and are you taking any other medications?
[patient] just the ibuprofen.
[doctor] okay. um, and have you had any surgeries in the past?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay. um, and what about allergies? any allergies to medications?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay. um, and have you had any other symptoms like this in the past?
[patient] no, nothing like this.
[doctor] okay. um, okay. well, let me just check your hand real quick. so, um, i'm just gon na check your hand. i'm gon na press on your wrist and see if it hurts. so, um, this is your wrist. and i'm just gon na press on it. does that hurt?
[patient] yeah, it does.
[doctor] okay. um, and then i'm gon na press on this side. and does that hurt?
[patient] yeah, that hurts too.
[doctor] okay. um, and then i'm gon na press on this side. and does that hurt?
[patient] no, that doesn't hurt.
[doctor] okay. um, okay. and then i'm gon na press on this side. and does that hurt?
[patient] no, that doesn't hurt.
[doctor] okay. um, okay. um, i'm just gon na check your fingers. so, um, i'm just gon na press on each of your fingers and see if it hurts. so, um, this one. does that hurt?
[patient] no, it doesn't.
[doctor] okay. um, this one?
[patient] no, it doesn't.
[doctor] okay. um, this one?
[patient] yeah, that hurts.
[doctor] okay. um, this one?
[patient] yeah, that hurts too.
[doctor] okay. um, this one?
[patient] yeah, that hurts too.
[doctor] okay. um, okay. um, i'm just gon na check your grip strength. so, um, i'm gon na squeeze your hand and see how hard you can squeeze me back. so, um, go ahead and squeeze my hand as hard as you can.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. um, and then i'm gon na squeeze you back. and can you feel my squeeze?
[patient] yeah, i can feel it.
[doctor] okay. um, and then i'm gon na squeeze you again. and can you feel my squeeze?
[patient] yeah, i can feel it.
[doctor] okay. um, and then i'm gon na squeeze you again. and can you feel my squeeze?
[patient] yeah, i can feel it.
[doctor] okay. um, okay. um, and then i'm gon na release. okay, so, um, your grip strength is a little weak on the right side. um, and then i'm gon na check your reflexes. so, um, i'm just gon na hit your wrist and see if you have a reflex. so, um, go ahead and relax your hand. and then i'm just gon na hit your wrist. and does that make your fingers move?
[patient] no, it doesn't.
[doctor] okay. um, okay. um, i'm just gon na check your reflexes on the other side. so, um, go ahead and relax your hand. and then i'm just gon na hit your wrist. and does that make your fingers move?
[patient] yeah, it does.
[doctor] okay. um, okay. um, so, i think what's going on is you have a condition called carpal tunnel syndrome. it's where the nerves in your wrist get compressed and it causes numbness and tingling in your hand. and it's probably caused by your job, where you're doing a lot of typing and using the mouse. um, but i think we can treat this conservatively. um, i'm gon na recommend that you wear a wrist splint at night, um, to help support your wrist and take some of the pressure off the nerves. and i'm also gon na increase your dose of ibuprofen to 600 milligrams, four times a day, to help with the inflammation. um, and then i'm gon na order an emg nerve conduction study to see how severe the compression is. um, if that shows that the compression is severe, then we might need to consider surgery. but i think we can try the conservative treatment first and see how you do. um, and i'm also gon na recommend that you try to take regular breaks and stretch your hand and wrist. um, and try to avoid using the mouse and keyboard for long periods of time.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay?
[patient] yeah, that sounds good.
[doctor] okay. um, and do you have any questions for me?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay. um, okay. well, i'm gon na go ahead and order those tests and we'll go from there. okay?
[patient] okay. sounds good.
[doctor] okay. um, and do you have any questions for me?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay. um, okay. well, i'll talk to you soon.
[patient] okay. }}<|eot_id|>
