dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, ms. thompson. how are you?
[patient] i'm doing alright. i'm a little bit sore from swimming a lot more lately.
[doctor] okay. so you're doing some swimming. how's that going?
[patient] it's going good. i've been trying to get out more.
[doctor] okay. and how's your back doing?
[patient] my back's been doing well. i've been doing okay. i've been trying to get outside more and it's been helping my depression a lot.
[doctor] okay. that's good. and how about your carpal tunnel? how's that doing?
[patient] it's doing really well. i've been getting full range of motion and it feels a lot better.
[doctor] okay. great. so, what brings you in today?
[patient] i've been having some knee pain.
[doctor] okay. yeah. have you hurt your knee or anything?
[patient] no, i just started doing more swimming and i've been doing a lot of laps and my knee just starts hurting after a couple of laps. i can only do about 5 laps now and i could do about 10 before.
[doctor] okay. have you fallen or anything?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. all right. so, i'm gon na go ahead and do a quick physical exam. so, your vitals are fine. your blood pressure is 120 over 80, your heart rate is 70. your neck is nice and supple. your lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. you have a regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, gallops or rubs. no extra heart sounds. your musculoskeletal exam is negative for lower extremity edema. pain with patellar inhibition, pain with range of motion of the right knee. and your neurological exam is appropriate mood and affect. grips are equal bilaterally. and on your review of systems, you endorsed right knee joint pain and chronic back pain. you endorsed depression. all right. so, i'm gon na go ahead and look at your x-ray. your x-ray of your right knee is unremarkable. all right. so, i'm gon na go ahead and tell you what's going on. so, i think you have chondromalacia of your right patella. it's a pretty common condition where the cartilage on the back of your knee starts to wear out and it can cause pain. it's pretty common in swimmers because they tend to bend their knees a lot when they're swimming. so, i'm gon na prescribe you some ibuprofen 600 milligrams four times a day with food. i also want you to do some straight leg raising exercises to strengthen your quadriceps. that will help with the pain. i also want you to try to do some activities that are less straining on your joints, like swimming freestyle laps or stop activity all together. if your pain does not improve in a couple of weeks, we'll follow up. i also want you to continue swimming regularly once your knee is feeling better. all right. so, i'm gon na go ahead and tell you about your back. your back is stable and well-controlled at this time. i think we can keep you on the same regimen that you're on now. and then, i'm gon na talk to you about your depression. you're doing well with your current regimen. we'll continue you on the same medications. and then, i'm gon na talk to you about your carpal tunnel. you're doing well. we'll continue you on the same regimen with no changes at this time. any questions?
[patient] no, that's good.
[doctor] okay. well, i'll go ahead and get you set up with the ibuprofen. and then, we'll go ahead and get you checked out. }}<|eot_id|>
