dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, emily, how are you?
[patient] i'm good. how are you?
[doctor] good. so i know you're here for your annual follow up. how are you doing?
[patient] i'm doing good.
[doctor] okay. so, i know you're trying to join the military and i know that they have some requirements for physical fitness and stuff like that. and they're saying you need to be able to do that without using any inhalers. so i know you're trying to wean off of the wixela and see how you do.
[patient] yeah, that's right.
[doctor] okay. so, you've been using the wixela and you've been using the albuterol before you run and you've been doing that a few times a week. but then on the other days, you're just using the wixela once a day.
[patient] yeah, that's right.
[doctor] okay. and you're feeling like that's working well for you?
[patient] yeah, it is.
[doctor] okay. so, let's talk a little bit about your asthma. i know you were using the symbicort before, but you were switched to the wixela because of insurance changes. how are you feeling about that?
[patient] i'm doing good.
[doctor] okay. and then you also have your seasonal allergies, you know, in the spring, summer, and fall, you use the zyrtec and that seems to work well for you.
[patient] yeah, that's right.
[doctor] okay. and then you also have your peanut and tree nut allergies. so, i know you've been trying to avoid those. have you had any accidental exposures?
[patient] no, not really.
[doctor] okay. good. and you're doing okay with the almonds and cashews?
[patient] yeah, i'm good with those.
[doctor] okay. great. so, i know you're trying to join the military and i know that they're going to be doing some physical fitness testing. and you're trying to show them that you can do that without using any inhalers. so, have you had any issues with your asthma or your allergies during that testing?
[patient] no, i have not.
[doctor] okay. good. so, let's talk a little bit about your nebulizer. you have one of those at home. have you had to use it recently?
[patient] no, i have not.
[doctor] okay. good. so, let's talk a little bit about your smoking and vaping. i know you were smoking and vaping a little bit, but you're trying to cut back on that. how's that going?
[patient] i'm trying.
[doctor] okay. well, let's talk a little bit about your zyrtec. so, you use that during the spring and fall. do you have to use it during the summer?
[patient] no, i do not.
[doctor] okay. so, you only use it during the spring and fall, and then you use it when you work outside?
[patient] yeah, that's right.
[doctor] okay. and how often do you use it when you work outside?
[patient] i use it three times a week.
[doctor] okay. and it seems to work well for you?
[patient] yeah, it does.
[doctor] okay. so, let's talk a little bit about your peanut allergy. so, i know you were exposed to peanuts last year and you had some contact hives. how's that going?
[patient] no, i have not had any issues with that.
[doctor] okay. good. so, you're doing okay with avoiding those?
[patient] yeah, i'm doing good.
[doctor] okay. and you're always keeping your epi pen with you?
[patient] yeah, i do.
[doctor] okay. and let's talk a little bit about your tree nut allergy. so, you know, you were able to try the almonds and you were able to tolerate those okay. but you're still avoiding all tree nuts?
[patient] yeah, i am.
[doctor] okay. so, let's talk a little bit about your past medical history. so, you have a history of asthma and allergic rhinitis and you have a history of food allergies to peanuts and tree nuts. is that correct?
[patient] yeah, that's right.
[doctor] okay. so, let's talk a little bit about your current medications. so, you're currently taking the wixela and you're using the albuterol and you have your epi pen. is that correct?
[patient] yeah, that's right.
[doctor] okay. so, let's talk a little bit about your test results. so, your asthma control test, your act score is 21, which is a good score. and your current food skin test sensitivities include peanuts. is that correct?
[patient] yeah, that's right.
[doctor] okay. so, let's talk a little bit about your assessment. so, i'm going to go ahead and assess you with moderate persistent asthma, uncomplicated. and you have allergic rhinitis. and then i'm also going to go ahead and assess you with an anaphylactic reaction due to peanuts. is that correct?
[patient] yeah, that's right.
[doctor] okay. so, let's talk a little bit about your plan. so, for your asthma, i'm going to go ahead and prescribe you the alvesco 80 mcg, 2 puffs, twice daily. and i want you to use that as needed. and then if you need something extra, you can use the albuterol. and then for your allergic rhinitis, i want you to continue the zyrtec 10 mg daily as needed. and then for your anaphylactic reaction due to peanuts, i want you to continue to avoid peanuts. and i'm going to go ahead and refill your epi pen today. is that okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. so, let's talk a little bit about your instructions. so, for your asthma, i want you to start the alvesco and use that as needed. and then if you need something extra, you can use the albuterol. and then for your allergic rhinitis, i want you to continue the zyrtec. and then for your anaphylactic reaction due to peanuts, i want you to continue to avoid peanuts. and then i want you to come back in four months to repeat the act and spirometry testing. is that okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. great. so, let's talk a little bit about your next steps. so, i want you to come back in four months to repeat the act and spirometry testing. and then we'll go from there. okay?
