[patient] no, i'm not able to walk without any assistance.
[doctor] okay. and are you able to walk to the living room?
[patient] no, i'm not able to walk to the living room.
[doctor] okay. so, you're able to walk to the dining room, but not the living room?
[patient] no, i'm not able to walk to the living room.
[doctor] okay. so, i'm gon na go ahead and review your vitals. your blood pressure is 124 over 80, your heart rate is 64, your respiratory rate is 18, and your weight is 174 pounds. and your pain is 0 out of 10. and then i'm gon na go ahead and do a physical exam.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. so, on your physical exam, i'm gon na start with your constitutional exam. you're a pleasant and cooperative man who is able to converse easily. and then on your head and face exam, the shunt site is clean, dry, and intact and confirmed at a setting of two. and then on your neurological exam, your mental status is intact. you have a slight improvement in your memory, but you still have some difficulty with remote memory. you were able to get two out of three memory words with cuing and the third one with multiple choice. this is a slight improvement over your initial score of 23 out of 30 with calculations and 24 out of 30 with spelling. and on your gait and balance, you were tested using the tinetti assessment tool, you were tested without an assistive device and you received a gait score of six to eight out of 12 and a balance score of 12 out of 16 for a total score of 18 to 20 out of 28. this has slightly improved from your initial score of 15 to 17 out of 28. and then on your cranial nerves, your pupils are equal, your extraocular movements are intact, your face is symmetric, and there is no dysarthria. and then on your motor exam, your motor is normal for bulk and strength. and on your coordination exam, your coordination is slow for finger-to-nose.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. so, i reviewed your ct scan from 10/15/2020, and it shows a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of munro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with the span of 10 mm. and by my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with just a tiny subdural collection. this was not noticed by the radiologist who stated no extra axial fluid collections. and there is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and then for your assessment, i think you have made some improvement with your hydrocephalus since your shunt surgery. there is a tiny fluid collection in the right parietal region. i do not believe that this tiny amount of fluid is symptomatic, and it was not documented by the radiologist when he read the ct scan.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and then for your plan, i think we should leave the setting at two for another three months before we consider changing the shunt. and then for your instructions, i want you to follow up in three months. and then i think that's all.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. any questions?
[patient] no, i do not have any questions.
[doctor] okay. }}<|eot_id|>
