dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, matthew. how are you?
[patient] i'm doing well. thanks. i'm just here for my right shoulder injury. i'm a little sore.
[doctor] okay. so, how did you hurt your shoulder?
[patient] i was playing football, and i got tackled a couple of times and landed on my right shoulder.
[doctor] okay. did you get up and continue playing?
[patient] yeah, i did.
[doctor] okay. and then you've had pain since then?
[patient] yeah, i have. it's mostly on the back of my shoulder.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any numbness or tingling in your fingers?
[patient] no, not at all.
[doctor] okay. and your strength in your hand is okay?
[patient] it's okay. i just don't want to lift anything too heavy.
[doctor] okay. can you touch your shoulder to your ear?
[patient] no, that's very painful.
[doctor] okay. and have you been taking any medications for the pain?
[patient] i've been taking tylenol.
[doctor] okay. and has that helped with the pain?
[patient] no, it really hasn't.
[doctor] okay. have you had any neck problems or any other joint problems?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any previous injuries to your right shoulder?
[patient] no, not that i'm aware of.
[doctor] okay. have you had a fever or chills since the injury?
[patient] no, i have not.
[doctor] okay. and did you hit your head at the time of the injury?
[patient] i did, but i didn't pass out.
[doctor] okay. so, i'm gon na go ahead and examine your neck and your shoulder. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] so, on your physical exam, your neck examination is normal. there's no tenderness to palpation. your range of motion is full. your head examination is normal. there is no trauma. there is no ecchymosis or bruising. on your right shoulder, i do appreciate pain to palpation of the posterior shoulder at the acromioclavicular joint. your range of motion is limited to about 90 degrees in abduction and adduction. there is pain with supination and pronation of the right arm. and you have a palpable radial artery pulse.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] so, your x-ray of your right shoulder reveals an acute acromioclavicular joint fracture.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] so, i'm gon na go ahead and explain what that means. we're gon na put you in a sling, and i'm gon na prescribe you meloxicam 15 milligrams once a day to help with your pain. i also think we should get a ct scan of your right shoulder to evaluate that further. i also recommend that we get you into formal physical therapy to strengthen your right shoulder in hopes of avoiding surgery in the future. and i think we should avoid tackle football for now. okay?
[patient] okay. that sounds like a plan.
[doctor] okay. any questions?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay. good. i'll go ahead and go ahead and order those tests and procedures, and we'll get you set up with physical therapy. and then we'll talk more about the ct scan and what we're gon na do next. okay?
[patient] sounds good. thank you.
[doctor] you're welcome. }}<|eot_id|>
