dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, teresa, how are you?
[patient] i'm doing okay. i'm having some pain in my right elbow.
[doctor] okay, well, let's talk a little bit about that. can you tell me more about the pain in your right elbow?
[patient] well, it started about 4 days ago. i don't know what caused it. i've been using the pottery wheel a lot more than usual. i've been making a bowl for my son.
[doctor] okay, that sounds like a fun project. yeah, the pottery wheel can be a bit rough on your elbow. have you noticed any specific movements that make it worse or better?
[patient] no, it's just kind of constant pain. it wakes me up at night if i roll over. i've been trying to ice it, but it doesn't really seem to help.
[doctor] okay, let's take a look. (pause) okay, so it's on the medial aspect of your elbow. and it radiates down your arm?
[patient] yes, that's right.
[doctor] okay. and it's a 7 out of 10 at its worst?
[patient] yes, that's right.
[doctor] okay. have you taken any ibuprofen or ice?
[patient] no, i haven't. i've just been trying to rest it.
[doctor] okay. okay. well, let's talk a little bit about your medical history. have you had any elbow injuries before?
[patient] no, not that i can remember.
[doctor] okay. and what about your hypertension and your diabetes? how are you managing those?
[patient] i've been checking my blood pressures regularly. they've been in the 110s to 130s. i'm taking lisinopril 10 mg daily. i've been taking it consistently.
[doctor] okay. great. and for your diabetes, how are you managing that?
[patient] sometimes i forget to take my metformin 500 mg twice a day. my blood sugars have been ranging from 150 to 170.
[doctor] okay. and do you do any exercise?
[patient] i try to walk 1 to 2 miles per day around my neighborhood. i also like to bake cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.
[doctor] okay, that sounds like fun. and do you enjoy working with ceramics?
[patient] yes, i do.
[doctor] okay. great. well, let's do a quick review of your systems. have you noticed any other symptoms?
[patient] no, just the elbow pain.
[doctor] okay. great. let's go ahead and take a look at your vitals. (pause) okay, your blood pressure is 120 over 70, your pulse is 72, your respiratory rate is 18, and your oxygen saturation is 100 percent on room air. your body temperature is 97.9 degrees. and your weight is 5 pounds up since last visit.
[doctor] okay. great. let's go ahead and do a physical exam. (pause) okay, your cardiovascular exam is normal. your respiratory exam is normal. no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi. your skin exam is normal. no rash or lesions. your musculoskeletal exam is positive for moderate tenderness at the medial epicondyle. pain with resisted pronation of your right forearm. pain with flexion and bending of your right wrist. no bruising or swelling. no discoloration or lesions.
[doctor] okay. let's go ahead and order some x-rays. (pause) okay, your x-ray of the right elbow is reviewed. it demonstrates no evidence of any fractures. no other abnormalities noted.
[doctor] okay, so let's talk a little bit about my assessment and plan. based on your examination and radiographic findings, i think you have medial epicondylitis. this is overuse of your elbow tendons, and it's causing pain. i'm going to recommend that you wear a sling on your right arm while you're awake to provide some support. i want you to ice your right elbow for 20 minutes, three times a day. and i want you to take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for 1 week.
[doctor] okay, that sounds like a good plan. and i also want you to hold off on creating pottery for the next 2 weeks to allow your arm to heal. if you continue to have persistent elbow pain, we may consider formal physical therapy at your next visit.
[doctor] okay, i also want to talk to you a little bit about your diabetes. i want you to check your blood sugars every day and record them. i'm going to increase your metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. i want you to take it consistently twice a day. and i'm going to order a hemoglobin a1c test to get a more accurate assessment of your long-term blood sugar levels.
[doctor] okay, and let's talk about your hypertension. you're currently taking lisinopril 10 mg. i want you to continue taking it. i also want you to continue with your walking routine.
[doctor] okay, any questions for me?
[patient] no, that's all.
[doctor] okay, great. i'll see you in 2 weeks to review the results of your hemoglobin a1c test and discuss your progress. }}<|eot_id|>
