dialogue is: {{ [doctor] okay, so good morning, alexis, how are you doing today?
[patient] i'm doing okay, i'm doing okay.
[doctor] yeah, all right. so, alexis, i see that you're here today for some right hand pain. can you tell me a little bit about what's going on with that?
[patient] yeah, so about six months ago, i started having some numbness and tingling in my first, second, and third fingers on my right hand. and then it's been constant over the past month or so.
[doctor] okay. and that's been constant? so it's not just intermittent?
[patient] no, it's constant.
[doctor] okay. and it's been constant for a month or so. and then what happens when you're working on a keyboard? does it get worse?
[patient] yeah, when i'm typing or using a mouse, it gets worse.
[doctor] okay. okay. and what about at night? do you have any trouble sleeping?
[patient] no, no, i don't.
[doctor] okay. okay. and what have you been doing for the pain? have you tried anything?
[patient] i've just been resting and taking ibuprofen.
[doctor] okay. okay. and that seems to help?
[patient] yeah, it does.
[doctor] okay. okay. so tell me a little bit about your job. what do you do?
[patient] i work on a keyboard all day.
[doctor] okay. okay. so you're on a keyboard all day. and how long have you been doing that?
[patient] um, about a year now.
[doctor] okay. okay. and have you noticed any other symptoms? any other pain or numbness in your body?
[patient] no, no, just my right hand.
[doctor] okay. okay. all right. so let's go ahead and do a quick physical exam. so, i'm just going to check your neck and your shoulders and your hand. (pause) okay, so on your physical exam, i do appreciate some pain with flexion and extension of your wrist. and your grip strength is decreased on the right compared to the left. and i do appreciate a positive tinel's sign.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and your neurovascular exam is intact. okay. so, alexis, i think that you have right carpal tunnel syndrome. and i know that this is a common condition where the median nerve is compressed as it goes through the wrist. and i think that's what's causing your symptoms. and i know you've been doing some things to help with the pain, but i think we need to do a little bit more. so i'm going to prescribe you some ibuprofen, 600 milligrams, four times a day. and i want you to wear a wrist splint at night to help with that pain and numbness. and i also want to order an emg nerve conduction study, just to confirm the diagnosis.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and if that's positive, we can discuss other treatment options, including physical therapy or surgical intervention with carpal tunnel release. but i think we can start with just the ibuprofen and the wrist splint. does that sound okay?
[patient] that sounds good.
[doctor] okay. okay. and i'll have my nurse come in and go over the discharge instructions with you.
[patient] okay. }}<|eot_id|>
