[patient] i've been taking ibuprofen.
[doctor] okay, and how much pain are you having?
[patient] it's pretty sore.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any numbness or tingling in your foot?
[patient] no, nothing like that.
[doctor] okay. great. and have you had any other injuries to that ankle before?
[patient] no, nothing like that.
[doctor] okay. great. so, gregory, i'm gon na go ahead and do a physical exam on you. and i'm gon na call out some things as i do it. so, on your examination of your right ankle, i do appreciate some ecchymosis, which means some bruising. i do appreciate some swelling over the lateral malleolus. and there is some tenderness to palpation anterolaterally in the soft tissue. there is no laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. and there is no bony tenderness on palpation of the foot. neurovascular is intact. capillary refill is less than 3 seconds with a strong dorsalis pedis pulse. and sensation is intact to light touch. so, i'm gon na go ahead and order some x-rays of your right ankle. and i'm gon na order them now. and i'm gon na have the nurse bring them in. and we'll go ahead and review them together. so, i'm gon na go ahead and review the results of your x-rays. and they did not show any signs of a fracture. so, it looks like you've got a right ankle sprain. so, i'm gon na go ahead and recommend that you keep your right lower extremity elevated, especially when seated. you can also continue to ice your right ankle. i'm gon na give you an aircast to help stabilize your ankle. you can use crutches for the next one to two days, and then follow with weight-bearing as tolerated. and i'm gon na have you continue to take some nsaids as needed for pain control. and i want you to follow up with me in two weeks to see how you're doing and to determine your return to play date. so, do you have any questions for me?
